1
00:00:02,650 --> 00:00:03,850
[SPEAKER_02]: So, how is it going now?

2
00:00:03,870 --> 00:00:04,791
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you start...?

3
00:00:04,811 --> 00:00:19,395
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, actually... Actually, first thing I want to know is, actually, many monks, they ask me, the... what's the problem, about their understanding of dependent origination.

4
00:00:19,635 --> 00:00:24,056
[SPEAKER_00]: Actually, I asked for Venerable Panyu, he said, it's not necessary for me to know it.

5
00:00:24,316 --> 00:00:26,057
[SPEAKER_00]: He thought that maybe...

6
00:00:26,760 --> 00:00:27,701
[SPEAKER_00]: I get confused.

7
00:00:28,301 --> 00:00:29,622
[SPEAKER_00]: But, eh...

8
00:00:29,822 --> 00:00:34,426
[SPEAKER_00]: I actually, simply, I don't understand what they're talking about.

9
00:00:34,446 --> 00:00:42,192
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, because I studied only the teaching of, you know, the timeless creation.

10
00:00:43,753 --> 00:00:44,394
[SPEAKER_00]: But, eh...

11
00:00:44,554 --> 00:00:49,117
[SPEAKER_00]: I think in Srilanka, most monks, they don't, eh... they don't understand that.

12
00:00:49,157 --> 00:00:51,239
[SPEAKER_00]: They think, like, it's a process.

13
00:00:54,330 --> 00:00:57,611
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's like two lines or three lines, I don't know.

14
00:00:57,631 --> 00:00:59,692
[SPEAKER_00]: What they're talking about, I don't understand.

15
00:00:59,972 --> 00:01:06,195
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's very difficult for me to explain what Venerable Nyanavilla taught.

16
00:01:06,215 --> 00:01:08,636
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how do you explain to them?

17
00:01:08,676 --> 00:01:17,499
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, I don't think... You have to start with a premise that they already have a different view.

18
00:01:17,519 --> 00:01:21,761
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't want to say they have a wrong view, but yeah, from Nyanavilla's point of view, that's a wrong view.

19
00:01:22,101 --> 00:01:22,561
[SPEAKER_00]: Wrong view.

20
00:01:23,477 --> 00:01:29,481
[SPEAKER_02]: Because they approach Patiṭṭhā Upādā as an explanation.

21
00:01:30,902 --> 00:01:35,084
[SPEAKER_02]: I remember Bhikkhu Bodhi, he was very, very much sharing the traditional view.

22
00:01:36,045 --> 00:01:39,607
[SPEAKER_02]: And, and he would write, write about it and so on.

23
00:01:39,807 --> 00:01:41,488
[SPEAKER_02]: And he would say that,

24
00:01:42,444 --> 00:01:45,767
[SPEAKER_02]: No, this is not... not the core of the dhamma as such.

25
00:01:45,807 --> 00:01:51,692
[SPEAKER_02]: This is more like an encompassing explanation of the entire teaching and the purpose of it.

26
00:01:52,613 --> 00:02:01,120
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in a way, when you look at it, well, okay, that... like, it's not wrong, because what... any aspect of the dhamma contains the entire dhamma.

27
00:02:01,781 --> 00:02:08,307
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can say, in relation to anything, that it encompasses the entire dhamma, because it can be enough for somebody to become an arahant.

28
00:02:09,300 --> 00:02:24,426
[SPEAKER_02]: However, the Buddha himself said, well, Sariputta said, dhamma is paticca-sampādha, paticca-sampādha is dhamma, dhamma-sīla, dhamma-sīla, which means, oh, no, it's actually quite... and then the Buddha also referred to paticca-sampādha as the reason.

29
00:02:24,986 --> 00:02:28,928
[SPEAKER_02]: Not knowing that is why people remain bound to saṃsara.

30
00:02:29,128 --> 00:02:34,570
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's actually not an explanation of some ideal, an overview.

31
00:02:34,690 --> 00:02:36,131
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a very direct insight.

32
00:02:37,111 --> 00:02:38,712
[SPEAKER_02]: And the fact is that

33
00:02:39,390 --> 00:02:49,836
[SPEAKER_02]: Every patiṣṭha upādāna formulation in the suttas starts first by saying, with arising... with this, this is, or with the arising of this, this arises.

34
00:02:50,317 --> 00:02:51,697
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's already akālika.

35
00:02:52,378 --> 00:02:54,899
[SPEAKER_02]: So, with the presence of this, this is present.

36
00:02:56,180 --> 00:03:08,895
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't say, as Nibiru pointed out, if you adopt the three-life interpretation, it will be with this arising of this, and then ceasing, then this arises, then it ceases, then something else arises, then it ceases, then something else...

37
00:03:09,335 --> 00:03:10,156
[SPEAKER_02]: But it doesn't say that.

38
00:03:10,216 --> 00:03:12,359
[SPEAKER_02]: It says with the arising of this, this is.

39
00:03:13,660 --> 00:03:20,565
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the determination, the reason, the cause, is present simultaneously with that which is being determined.

40
00:03:20,585 --> 00:03:23,367
[SPEAKER_02]: And then he would... the Buddha would say himself, with the arising of what?

41
00:03:23,567 --> 00:03:24,047
[SPEAKER_02]: This is.

42
00:03:24,207 --> 00:03:26,329
[SPEAKER_02]: And then he would formulate paticca upādāna.

43
00:03:27,029 --> 00:03:31,493
[SPEAKER_02]: And Bhikkhu Bodhi was aware of that, because, you know, his pāli is very good.

44
00:03:33,050 --> 00:03:37,053
[SPEAKER_02]: But he would... he would refuse to give up his view of three-life interpretation.

45
00:03:37,413 --> 00:03:44,918
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then he would dismiss this opening statement with this, this is, and say, ah, this is just an abstract principle.

46
00:03:45,839 --> 00:03:50,222
[SPEAKER_02]: But actually, even... even an abstract principle is still a principle.

47
00:03:51,223 --> 00:03:53,164
[SPEAKER_02]: So, whether it's an abstract or not, it's irrelevant.

48
00:03:53,524 --> 00:03:55,365
[SPEAKER_02]: But by saying abstract, you dismiss it.

49
00:03:57,178 --> 00:04:01,061
[SPEAKER_02]: But the fact is, it's a principle within which the whole dhamma is contained.

50
00:04:01,962 --> 00:04:12,771
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you dismiss that, and then your interpretation of that paticcaṁ pada goes against the principle, means that's a contradiction, and that's why it's wrong.

51
00:04:13,231 --> 00:04:20,077
[SPEAKER_02]: So, he always, always just, ah, this is just an abstract principle, and then puts it aside by labeling it such, and then goes on

52
00:04:20,785 --> 00:04:23,747
[SPEAKER_02]: into expounding how the feeling connection and this...

53
00:04:23,808 --> 00:04:26,009
[SPEAKER_02]: I even forgot what the traditional interpretation was.

54
00:04:26,029 --> 00:04:26,750
[SPEAKER_02]: Long time, Reddit.

55
00:04:27,290 --> 00:04:27,791
[SPEAKER_02]: But, yeah.

56
00:04:28,211 --> 00:04:30,393
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, you had other people said, well, it doesn't pertain...

57
00:04:30,613 --> 00:04:32,875
[SPEAKER_02]: I think that was K. Yananda there, I think.

58
00:04:33,235 --> 00:04:36,438
[SPEAKER_02]: He was saying, doesn't pertain to three life, it pertains to one life.

59
00:04:37,058 --> 00:04:39,240
[SPEAKER_02]: But, again, he took it as an explanation.

60
00:04:40,041 --> 00:04:43,123
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, as a kind of explaining what's happening.

61
00:04:44,044 --> 00:04:44,925
[SPEAKER_02]: That's not the dhamma.

62
00:04:44,945 --> 00:04:54,713
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, if you're explaining what's happening, then it loses the aspect of opanaiko, leading on, that the Buddha talked about.

63
00:04:55,613 --> 00:04:59,737
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if I provide you an explanation, you can say, oh, yes, or we can say, oh, no.

64
00:05:00,297 --> 00:05:00,778
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's it.

65
00:05:00,918 --> 00:05:01,678
[SPEAKER_02]: It stops there.

66
00:05:03,039 --> 00:05:05,381
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it just remains at the level of intellect and the view.

67
00:05:06,282 --> 00:05:08,064
[SPEAKER_02]: But if the Buddha describes something,

68
00:05:09,680 --> 00:05:14,342
[SPEAKER_02]: That in order for you to understand, it leads you on to more understanding.

69
00:05:15,003 --> 00:05:17,304
[SPEAKER_02]: That means, ah, that has the nature of the dhamma.

70
00:05:17,584 --> 00:05:22,826
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you have to bear that in mind when you try to explain to somebody who already has a different view.

71
00:05:23,767 --> 00:05:25,668
[SPEAKER_02]: There are very little chances of them understanding it.

72
00:05:26,468 --> 00:05:32,111
[SPEAKER_02]: Because... it is possible for them to understand, but it has to come from them.

73
00:05:32,791 --> 00:05:34,752
[SPEAKER_02]: In other words, they first have to see that they're wrong,

74
00:05:35,903 --> 00:05:41,565
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, you have to start looking out for what is less wrong, in terms of their current view.

75
00:05:42,105 --> 00:05:44,246
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, they might be recipient to hear it.

76
00:05:44,806 --> 00:05:48,947
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you're just trying to convince somebody otherwise, it's useless.

77
00:05:49,287 --> 00:05:50,047
[SPEAKER_02]: It won't work.

78
00:05:50,968 --> 00:06:04,812
[SPEAKER_00]: Venerable Varapanyo, he said, actually, if we try to understand the teaching of Venerable Ñānavīr, timeless relation, and then the past and the future, both are not important, he said.

79
00:06:05,665 --> 00:06:11,467
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a traditional interpretation program that past and the future... Oh, I see what you mean.

80
00:06:11,547 --> 00:06:11,947
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, yeah.

81
00:06:12,147 --> 00:06:12,668
[SPEAKER_02]: No, that's it.

82
00:06:12,768 --> 00:06:16,809
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, whether it's your past, whether it's your future, it's present as such.

83
00:06:18,610 --> 00:06:26,772
[SPEAKER_02]: Past is present, future is present, and the present is present, which is the nature of humanism.

84
00:06:26,812 --> 00:06:28,133
[SPEAKER_02]: It's immediacy and reflection.

85
00:06:29,951 --> 00:06:31,634
[SPEAKER_02]: Two modes of existence, so to speak.

86
00:06:32,275 --> 00:06:35,341
[SPEAKER_02]: So, when you think about your past, you think it in your present.

87
00:06:36,003 --> 00:06:38,387
[SPEAKER_02]: When you think about your present, you think it in your present.

88
00:06:38,487 --> 00:06:40,491
[SPEAKER_02]: When you think about your future, you think in the present.

89
00:06:41,601 --> 00:06:53,044
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you abandon that present, in terms of you fully understand it, then neither your past, nor future, nor that which is right in front of you, can pertain to you anymore.

90
00:06:53,825 --> 00:06:56,385
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in that sense, yeah, absolutely, it's not important anymore.

91
00:06:56,885 --> 00:07:04,588
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if the past would be important for the practice of the dhamma, well, you couldn't practice the dhamma, because you can't change what has happened.

92
00:07:06,501 --> 00:07:16,367
[SPEAKER_02]: If the future would be important in terms of how you just have to wait and it will happen by itself, well, then, again, you can never know that directly for yourself.

93
00:07:16,387 --> 00:07:20,890
[SPEAKER_02]: You can just take it on faith and believe, which is actually what most of religions rely on.

94
00:07:21,170 --> 00:07:24,832
[SPEAKER_02]: Just this belief that eventually it will all work out fine for me.

95
00:07:25,893 --> 00:07:27,794
[SPEAKER_02]: But, like, well, okay, good luck then.

96
00:07:27,834 --> 00:07:29,415
[SPEAKER_02]: But what makes you think so?

97
00:07:31,796 --> 00:07:32,276
[SPEAKER_02]: So, even if

98
00:07:35,592 --> 00:07:47,360
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, in the cases of people who had horrible past, like Angulimala or other, you know, if they can overturn that and escape that sort of past...

99
00:07:49,021 --> 00:07:51,522
[SPEAKER_02]: But he couldn't, certainly, he couldn't undo what he'd done.

100
00:07:53,143 --> 00:07:55,745
[SPEAKER_02]: And he'd done some serious things.

101
00:07:56,926 --> 00:08:02,109
[SPEAKER_02]: So you realize, ah, it's in the present, which is why I would have said it's here and now, visible here and now.

102
00:08:02,709 --> 00:08:03,710
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't have to wait for it.

103
00:08:04,093 --> 00:08:05,334
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, you don't have to remember it.

104
00:08:05,594 --> 00:08:06,355
[SPEAKER_02]: It's here and now.

105
00:08:06,475 --> 00:08:10,778
[SPEAKER_02]: And, in the same sense, he called it opanaika and called it timeless, not pertaining to time.

106
00:08:11,218 --> 00:08:12,779
[SPEAKER_02]: And, that's exactly how it doesn't pertain.

107
00:08:13,420 --> 00:08:16,282
[SPEAKER_02]: If it was pertaining to time, it would pertain to past.

108
00:08:16,662 --> 00:08:17,783
[SPEAKER_02]: It would require future.

109
00:08:18,883 --> 00:08:19,924
[SPEAKER_02]: But, it doesn't.

110
00:08:19,944 --> 00:08:25,028
[SPEAKER_02]: It's timeless, which means, ah... Because, if... if past is present, that's it.

111
00:08:25,508 --> 00:08:28,870
[SPEAKER_02]: One thing is present, like... how to put it?

112
00:08:29,010 --> 00:08:29,311
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay.

113
00:08:30,071 --> 00:08:32,293
[SPEAKER_02]: Past is in present.

114
00:08:33,577 --> 00:08:34,997
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's a timeless relationship.

115
00:08:36,238 --> 00:08:38,638
[SPEAKER_02]: Present is in the present.

116
00:08:39,658 --> 00:08:41,239
[SPEAKER_02]: That's another timeless relationship.

117
00:08:42,319 --> 00:08:45,520
[SPEAKER_02]: Future is, as future, in the present.

118
00:08:45,620 --> 00:08:47,080
[SPEAKER_02]: That's another timeless relationship.

119
00:08:47,820 --> 00:08:53,862
[SPEAKER_02]: When you look at it from the point of view of present, even if it's present what you're looking at, that's a timeless relationship.

120
00:08:55,970 --> 00:09:04,895
[SPEAKER_02]: So if you understand that, then whatever particularly was that past you were thinking of, or whatever particular thoughts were pertaining for the future, that's irrelevant.

121
00:09:06,095 --> 00:09:11,818
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you abandoned present, which means you abandoned past, future and present that were in the present.

122
00:09:12,979 --> 00:09:14,059
[SPEAKER_02]: So nothing is left out.

123
00:09:17,441 --> 00:09:23,544
[SPEAKER_02]: And in terms of not having to understand paticca upādāna, well, that's true in a sense.

124
00:09:23,764 --> 00:09:24,505
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't have to

125
00:09:26,642 --> 00:09:33,526
[SPEAKER_02]: Understand all of the individual factors, the way the Buddha laid them down, because they're quite subtle themselves.

126
00:09:34,486 --> 00:09:40,790
[SPEAKER_02]: But you need to understand this timeless principle, which is, which is why the Buddha called atiṣṇupāda dhamma dhamma patiṣṇupāda.

127
00:09:41,450 --> 00:09:42,731
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the same.

128
00:09:42,771 --> 00:09:50,535
[SPEAKER_02]: Actually, was it... One who sees the... One who sees the Buddha, sees the dhamma.

129
00:09:50,555 --> 00:09:51,836
[SPEAKER_02]: One who sees the dhamma, sees the Buddha.

130
00:09:52,957 --> 00:09:54,178
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that's what the Buddha said.

131
00:09:54,518 --> 00:10:00,022
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, Sariputra said, in terms of dhamma and paticampadaya, or Buddha or paticampadaya.

132
00:10:00,522 --> 00:10:01,383
[SPEAKER_01]: Mithushu said.

133
00:10:01,944 --> 00:10:02,224
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

134
00:10:03,164 --> 00:10:08,448
[SPEAKER_02]: Either way, you don't have to understand all the specific factors of it.

135
00:10:09,569 --> 00:10:13,612
[SPEAKER_02]: But you have to understand that principle that Bhikkhu Bodhi dismisses as abstract.

136
00:10:14,653 --> 00:10:17,235
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you understand that all of these things are implied.

137
00:10:18,856 --> 00:10:20,477
[SPEAKER_02]: And the fact that the Buddha

138
00:10:21,766 --> 00:10:25,389
[SPEAKER_02]: Quite often, eh... described the Tīṣṇhapāda differently.

139
00:10:26,369 --> 00:10:33,414
[SPEAKER_02]: Sometimes it would... it would have 12 factors, sometimes it would have 15 factors, sometimes it would stop at nāmarūpa, viññāṇa, viññāṇa, nāmarūpa.

140
00:10:33,895 --> 00:10:38,118
[SPEAKER_02]: He would omit the whole viññāṇa, sankhāra, and then sankhāra, avijjā.

141
00:10:38,138 --> 00:10:39,939
[SPEAKER_02]: He would just... he just wouldn't talk about it.

142
00:10:40,780 --> 00:10:48,445
[SPEAKER_02]: So you realize, oh, it's an exemplification of a... of a principle that can be laid out in... in different ways.

143
00:10:49,026 --> 00:10:50,807
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's still the same principle.

144
00:10:51,748 --> 00:11:06,562
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you dismiss the principle, you get bogged down in, well, in the detail, which becomes, basically, you have to explain it, you have to find the connections, and it becomes linear, psychological, traditional, commentarial.

145
00:11:07,062 --> 00:11:13,128
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you keep the principle in mind, as something that has been understood, then you might even not think in terms of

146
00:11:14,292 --> 00:11:16,973
[SPEAKER_02]: The way the Buddha laid it down, the whole Patitsangpa, it's irrelevant.

147
00:11:17,073 --> 00:11:20,175
[SPEAKER_02]: You understood the principle, and freed you from ignorance, and it's done.

148
00:11:20,735 --> 00:11:30,639
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, again, all of those individual factors, if you can refer to them as such, it's not 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8.

149
00:11:31,600 --> 00:11:36,142
[SPEAKER_02]: They're all present simultaneously, just on different layers.

150
00:11:37,603 --> 00:11:40,944
[SPEAKER_02]: Present on different layers within the same principle.

151
00:11:40,984 --> 00:11:42,185
[SPEAKER_02]: So it is all simultaneous.

152
00:11:43,536 --> 00:11:50,282
[SPEAKER_02]: But it doesn't mean that, again, you can approach it, you can think in terms of these twelve factors arising at the same time.

153
00:11:50,442 --> 00:11:51,583
[SPEAKER_02]: It depends how you look at it.

154
00:11:53,064 --> 00:11:54,806
[SPEAKER_02]: The principle arises at the same time.

155
00:11:54,866 --> 00:11:56,528
[SPEAKER_02]: With this, this is.

156
00:11:58,589 --> 00:12:04,495
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, if you take it further, you can start defining the same principle further and further and further.

157
00:12:05,936 --> 00:12:08,538
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's how those factors were defined.

158
00:12:11,093 --> 00:12:12,533
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's not... it's not more than that.

159
00:12:12,613 --> 00:12:21,135
[SPEAKER_02]: And certainly, it's not about, like, drawing a linear connection between the factors and trying to present a chart that will really encompass it all.

160
00:12:21,396 --> 00:12:29,778
[SPEAKER_02]: Just... that's kind of... well, that is definitely pointless and won't... won't bring any... well, any timeless understanding.

161
00:12:34,239 --> 00:12:39,160
[SPEAKER_02]: And I think, in the end, I will also mention in one of the letters, when somebody asking, well, like,

162
00:12:40,708 --> 00:12:46,170
[SPEAKER_02]: This is the first time that they heard that somebody defines pāṇḍa like that, and so on.

163
00:12:46,990 --> 00:12:49,271
[SPEAKER_02]: They were not disagreeing with him, but they were just curious.

164
00:12:49,291 --> 00:12:50,091
[SPEAKER_02]: How's that?

165
00:12:51,172 --> 00:12:59,335
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, well, like, you know, you might have another sort of pāṇḍa who wouldn't necessarily think in those terms.

166
00:12:59,895 --> 00:13:06,157
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if he didn't approach it through that way, in terms of understanding, he won't be able to recognize

167
00:13:07,195 --> 00:13:07,856
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what it is.

168
00:13:08,156 --> 00:13:11,599
[SPEAKER_02]: But what he will recognize, what he will know, is the principle.

169
00:13:11,719 --> 00:13:13,000
[SPEAKER_02]: With this, this is.

170
00:13:14,341 --> 00:13:18,344
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's, that's, that principle is contained in, in that, like, eh...

171
00:13:19,791 --> 00:13:30,036
[SPEAKER_02]: The representative insight of a sotāpanna, which is whatever has the nature of arising, for that reason, has the nature of ceasing, because it has arisen.

172
00:13:31,316 --> 00:13:33,237
[SPEAKER_02]: So, permanence is implied.

173
00:13:33,537 --> 00:13:36,739
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's how the being gets undone.

174
00:13:37,679 --> 00:13:45,103
[SPEAKER_02]: So, again, it's not that, you know, for sotāpanna, for anārahaṃ, those twelve factors just get kind of

175
00:13:45,977 --> 00:13:49,358
[SPEAKER_02]: ...emerged like a, you know, glowing writing on the wall or something like that.

176
00:13:49,498 --> 00:13:52,218
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a principle that can be taken as far as necessary.

177
00:13:53,038 --> 00:14:12,582
[SPEAKER_02]: Obviously, the Buddha being the foremost of all of the Buddhas in explaining the dhamma, he really explained it to the last every possible detail, which is why the previous Buddha, when he explained the paticca upādāna, he actually didn't go further than nāmarūpa viññāṇa, viññāṇa nāmarūpa.

178
00:14:13,843 --> 00:14:15,383
[SPEAKER_02]: And again, if you end up

179
00:14:15,848 --> 00:14:24,992
[SPEAKER_02]: If you end the description of the principle, you end with mutual dependence of two things.

180
00:14:25,853 --> 00:14:29,855
[SPEAKER_02]: And two things can be mutually dependent only if they're simultaneously present.

181
00:14:30,775 --> 00:14:40,680
[SPEAKER_02]: If two sticks hold each other, which was the simile Sariputta gave for nāmarūpa determining viññāṇa, viññāṇa determining nāmarūpa, they have to be up simultaneously.

182
00:14:41,553 --> 00:14:44,336
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't have now one stick, then put it down, and then leave the other one.

183
00:14:44,436 --> 00:14:45,777
[SPEAKER_02]: There is no dependence there.

184
00:14:47,959 --> 00:14:58,870
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the entire relationship of whichever factor of participant you pick up, is within that nature, shares that nature of simultaneous dependence.

185
00:15:00,812 --> 00:15:05,196
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's a... consciousness is a really important thing, but...

186
00:15:06,163 --> 00:15:13,428
[SPEAKER_00]: Actually, before, many Christian people, they believe, like, consciousness is like a self or something, like, that is I am.

187
00:15:13,789 --> 00:15:14,890
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

188
00:15:16,631 --> 00:15:34,484
[SPEAKER_00]: Actually, after, after, actually, knowing the teaching of Venerable Jñānavīla, and then, actually, after meditating, actually, come to see, actually, this consciousness is just, like, supporting the, like, contact to arisen.

189
00:15:36,130 --> 00:15:37,871
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, it's one of the aggregates.

190
00:15:38,571 --> 00:15:45,895
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if somebody is out of nibbāna, full understanding, all of the aggregates need to be understood.

191
00:15:46,475 --> 00:15:49,117
[SPEAKER_02]: Obviously, consciousness is that which doesn't appear.

192
00:15:50,217 --> 00:15:57,021
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you can't see consciousness directly as an object, as you can see a cup, or a glass, or a door, or a car.

193
00:15:57,241 --> 00:15:59,702
[SPEAKER_02]: It just doesn't arise like that.

194
00:16:00,442 --> 00:16:04,825
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is why, most often, consciousness is that which people regard as their self.

195
00:16:06,049 --> 00:16:24,562
[SPEAKER_02]: But the fact that there is something, even if you don't know what it is, that there is a designation which you can call consciousness, you can say, and you can even describe it, means there is something in your experience that stands for that.

196
00:16:26,688 --> 00:16:31,132
[SPEAKER_02]: So, full understanding of that would mean full understanding of consciousness.

197
00:16:32,093 --> 00:16:39,701
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can't understand consciousness in the same way you would understand objects of your senses, because it precedes them.

198
00:16:39,881 --> 00:16:43,184
[SPEAKER_02]: It's because you're conscious, objects of your senses can arise.

199
00:16:44,705 --> 00:16:49,970
[SPEAKER_02]: If you are not conscious, eyes won't be seeing, ears won't be hearing.

200
00:16:52,442 --> 00:16:55,385
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's a... it's a... it's a negative phenomenon.

201
00:16:55,785 --> 00:17:10,618
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's... that's why, when I... when I... when I told you before, it's very important to... to recognize this actual and peripheral we spoke, because consciousness is the ultimate peripheral.

202
00:17:11,739 --> 00:17:13,981
[SPEAKER_02]: It's that peripheral that never appears, but it's there.

203
00:17:15,986 --> 00:17:25,154
[SPEAKER_02]: And the most precisely you can define it would be in the very ambiguous terms, such as it's the presence.

204
00:17:26,915 --> 00:17:27,776
[SPEAKER_02]: Presence of things.

205
00:17:28,396 --> 00:17:30,859
[SPEAKER_02]: The fact that things are present means consciousness is there.

206
00:17:33,020 --> 00:17:36,063
[SPEAKER_02]: Obviously, people can now overdo that.

207
00:17:36,722 --> 00:17:52,795
[SPEAKER_02]: And then end up mystifying consciousness, as the example you gave Christians, or New Age, whatever religion really, including Buddhism, when they say, oh, it's the ultimate consciousness, it's the pure consciousness, it's that consciousness, it's this consciousness.

208
00:17:53,395 --> 00:17:57,138
[SPEAKER_02]: You over-mystify that presence and then you start talking about presence in itself.

209
00:17:57,939 --> 00:18:03,463
[SPEAKER_02]: But if there's nothing present, you can't have the presence.

210
00:18:03,583 --> 00:18:05,865
[SPEAKER_02]: You need to have a thing in order for it to appear.

211
00:18:07,250 --> 00:18:09,451
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's why consciousness is not independent.

212
00:18:09,571 --> 00:18:11,172
[SPEAKER_02]: It requires nāmarūpa.

213
00:18:12,513 --> 00:18:15,655
[SPEAKER_02]: If there isn't, consciousness could not be discerned.

214
00:18:18,496 --> 00:18:29,182
[SPEAKER_02]: So, as much as consciousness determines that which appears, allows it to appear, in the same... to the same extent, that which has appeared defines consciousness.

215
00:18:29,662 --> 00:18:30,763
[SPEAKER_02]: Otherwise, it wouldn't be there.

216
00:18:32,563 --> 00:18:34,805
[SPEAKER_00]: Consciousness is just of six kinds and...

217
00:18:35,838 --> 00:18:42,161
[SPEAKER_02]: In particular, you don't have to think of them at the same time, because you get complicated things, but you can take a sense.

218
00:18:42,822 --> 00:18:44,042
[SPEAKER_02]: Eyes, ears, nose.

219
00:18:44,943 --> 00:18:46,003
[SPEAKER_02]: Eyes are conscious.

220
00:18:46,863 --> 00:18:48,925
[SPEAKER_02]: Ears are conscious.

221
00:18:48,945 --> 00:18:50,305
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the thing.

222
00:18:50,825 --> 00:18:57,189
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, most people automatically assume matter is out there, consciousness is in here.

223
00:18:58,969 --> 00:19:02,251
[SPEAKER_02]: But actually, matter and consciousness are on the same level.

224
00:19:03,372 --> 00:19:03,872
[SPEAKER_02]: Out there.

225
00:19:05,748 --> 00:19:08,149
[SPEAKER_02]: And their relationship is known as nāmarūpa.

226
00:19:09,729 --> 00:19:12,550
[SPEAKER_02]: And that can have the internal aspect and external aspect.

227
00:19:13,331 --> 00:19:20,573
[SPEAKER_02]: So, actually, consciousness is out there with the things.

228
00:19:20,613 --> 00:19:22,454
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is why it can never be yourself.

229
00:19:23,114 --> 00:19:26,255
[SPEAKER_02]: For as long as you think it's in here, you will be taking it as me.

230
00:19:27,496 --> 00:19:31,397
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you see it out there, in that material world,

231
00:19:32,813 --> 00:19:38,617
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, you realize, well, I cannot have that, nothing more than I could have matter to be mine.

232
00:19:40,879 --> 00:19:57,451
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's what Yanira was talking about in slightly different terms, where he was saying that rūpa and viññāṇa, they're, eh... they're like juxt... well, superimposed, or juxtaposed, like two different things, but they come together.

233
00:19:58,692 --> 00:19:59,313
[SPEAKER_02]: They don't mix.

234
00:19:59,753 --> 00:20:01,835
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, they just get dissociated.

235
00:20:03,826 --> 00:20:05,968
[SPEAKER_02]: The result of that is nāmarūpa.

236
00:20:06,368 --> 00:20:10,212
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the whole world that you know, that you can never know your past, your future.

237
00:20:10,352 --> 00:20:14,096
[SPEAKER_02]: It's all within nāmarūpa, within the conscious matter.

238
00:20:17,339 --> 00:20:21,483
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if your consciousness is truly in you, it would be independent from the body.

239
00:20:23,154 --> 00:20:26,696
[SPEAKER_02]: But when that material body gets disintegrated, consciousness goes.

240
00:20:27,137 --> 00:20:27,917
[SPEAKER_02]: It cannot stay.

241
00:20:28,057 --> 00:20:29,638
[SPEAKER_00]: You mean that, like a...

242
00:20:29,919 --> 00:20:30,519
[SPEAKER_02]: Sense of self.

243
00:20:30,819 --> 00:20:32,100
[SPEAKER_00]: Sense of self.

244
00:20:32,180 --> 00:20:34,722
[SPEAKER_00]: But the consciousness is in the body, you know, I think... No.

245
00:20:35,302 --> 00:20:35,522
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

246
00:20:35,903 --> 00:20:36,143
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

247
00:20:36,843 --> 00:20:37,504
[SPEAKER_02]: Why would it be?

248
00:20:39,745 --> 00:20:43,708
[SPEAKER_00]: Without consciousness, this body can't... Consciousness is associated with the body.

249
00:20:43,888 --> 00:20:44,488
[SPEAKER_00]: Associated.

250
00:20:44,508 --> 00:20:44,689
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

251
00:20:45,229 --> 00:20:45,669
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you...

252
00:20:45,969 --> 00:20:52,354
[SPEAKER_02]: The only reason you can look in or out, or in and out, or wherever you want, is because you're already conscious.

253
00:20:54,544 --> 00:21:01,452
[SPEAKER_02]: So that the sense of space, the direction where you look, is possible only because the body is already conscious.

254
00:21:02,293 --> 00:21:04,535
[SPEAKER_02]: The consciousness, the five aggregates are already there.

255
00:21:05,336 --> 00:21:06,657
[SPEAKER_02]: So it's secondary to that.

256
00:21:09,180 --> 00:21:15,187
[SPEAKER_02]: If it's secondary, it means you can't use it then to find its origin, because it's a result of the origin, not the origin.

257
00:21:18,426 --> 00:21:24,948
[SPEAKER_02]: And if you, you know, you look in the distance and you see the tree really really far out, well, your conscience is out there.

258
00:21:27,709 --> 00:21:29,209
[SPEAKER_00]: Consciousness is out there means that...

259
00:21:29,729 --> 00:21:31,670
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't mean it's in that matter.

260
00:21:32,150 --> 00:21:38,052
[SPEAKER_02]: It means it's associated with that matter, no matter how far it is, whether it's in your thoughts or through your senses.

261
00:21:39,492 --> 00:21:40,553
[SPEAKER_02]: It's actually external.

262
00:21:43,574 --> 00:21:46,495
[SPEAKER_02]: For arahant, all of the aggregates become external.

263
00:21:47,135 --> 00:21:47,635
[SPEAKER_00]: External.

264
00:21:47,876 --> 00:21:48,376
[SPEAKER_02]: None of it.

265
00:21:48,737 --> 00:21:53,739
[SPEAKER_00]: External means... you mean that not self in the... Well, yes, for arahant, yes.

266
00:21:53,999 --> 00:21:54,240
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

267
00:21:55,220 --> 00:21:56,501
[SPEAKER_02]: External means not self.

268
00:21:56,881 --> 00:22:11,249
[SPEAKER_02]: And the Buddha did say that for somebody completely free from ignorance, what is left is this conscious body and all whatever name and form externally.

269
00:22:12,389 --> 00:22:13,070
[SPEAKER_02]: That's what's left.

270
00:22:14,299 --> 00:22:27,145
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the body that is associated with the consciousness or consciousness associated with the body, it's just like two completely different things that kind of come and stop together, which is the birth.

271
00:22:28,145 --> 00:22:31,307
[SPEAKER_02]: But then, when one of them goes, the other one cannot remain standing.

272
00:22:31,907 --> 00:22:39,130
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's not that consciousness enters in the body, because any notion of space is a result of the fact that you're conscious.

273
00:22:40,791 --> 00:22:43,052
[SPEAKER_02]: So, consciousness cannot be in its own result.

274
00:22:45,289 --> 00:22:51,055
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, Arahant said that any idea appears that, like, as my body, but the thought itself, it's not his.

275
00:22:51,695 --> 00:23:03,887
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, even when he thinks, my, I, I am, or I'm this, or this is my cup, or whatever, that doesn't imply the emotional significance of me and sense of self.

276
00:23:04,588 --> 00:23:09,853
[SPEAKER_02]: In other words, I can say that's my cup, but if somebody comes to steal it, I won't suffer on account of it.

277
00:23:11,175 --> 00:23:14,317
[SPEAKER_02]: But as a designation of speech, you can, you know, and the Buddha said that.

278
00:23:14,377 --> 00:23:16,118
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, the Tathāgata says, I am.

279
00:23:16,618 --> 00:23:19,160
[SPEAKER_02]: But it doesn't mean that he really thinks he is.

280
00:23:19,180 --> 00:23:21,401
[SPEAKER_02]: He's just a figure of speech becomes for him.

281
00:23:22,461 --> 00:23:26,644
[SPEAKER_02]: Where a Kṛṭṭhūjana, when he says, I am, for him, that is his sense of self.

282
00:23:28,630 --> 00:23:30,270
[SPEAKER_02]: So, as a designation, it's valid.

283
00:23:30,410 --> 00:23:31,851
[SPEAKER_02]: You have to designate it somehow.

284
00:23:32,771 --> 00:23:34,971
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just a misunderstanding that goes with it.

285
00:23:35,572 --> 00:23:37,732
[SPEAKER_02]: That's removed, in case of an arahant.

286
00:23:37,972 --> 00:23:41,453
[SPEAKER_00]: It means thought itself is different, actually different, no?

287
00:23:41,473 --> 00:23:47,194
[SPEAKER_02]: I think... Well, different in as much that there is no greed, aversion, delusion, ever, again, to arise.

288
00:23:47,214 --> 00:23:47,834
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't appear.

289
00:23:48,254 --> 00:23:49,635
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, they cannot appear.

290
00:23:50,215 --> 00:23:50,635
[SPEAKER_00]: Cannot.

291
00:23:50,875 --> 00:23:51,055
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

292
00:23:51,335 --> 00:23:55,496
[SPEAKER_02]: So, he's not just temporarily free from greed, aversion, delusion, an arahant.

293
00:23:56,302 --> 00:24:01,244
[SPEAKER_02]: He's free from a possibility of those things arising, which is why he's an Arahant.

294
00:24:02,825 --> 00:24:16,430
[SPEAKER_02]: So, whenever there might be pleasing thoughts, displeasing thoughts, neutral thoughts, but pleasing thoughts won't be inducing any more lust, displeasing thoughts won't be inducing any anger, and neutral thoughts won't be inducing any delusion.

295
00:24:20,731 --> 00:24:23,773
[SPEAKER_00]: Displeasing, how it happens in the mind environment?

296
00:24:24,053 --> 00:24:24,653
[SPEAKER_02]: Displeasing.

297
00:24:26,053 --> 00:24:26,233
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

298
00:24:26,753 --> 00:24:27,914
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, this is disagreeable.

299
00:24:28,434 --> 00:24:29,975
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a... it's a feeling.

300
00:24:30,636 --> 00:24:36,099
[SPEAKER_02]: I actually didn't understand what... Well, I know, because if you could see the difference, you would be on the way to become an arahant.

301
00:24:36,379 --> 00:24:41,662
[SPEAKER_02]: How can something be displeasing without implying ill will or aversion?

302
00:24:42,523 --> 00:24:51,388
[SPEAKER_02]: If you can see that, then, oh, keep doing it, and then you learn how to be displeased without being angry, which means you free yourself from anger.

303
00:24:55,190 --> 00:24:55,450
[SPEAKER_02]: But...

304
00:24:56,850 --> 00:25:09,419
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't, like, feeling as feeling, for a puthujjana, is not the same when... well, it's not the same as arahant's feeling, as an aggregate.

305
00:25:10,019 --> 00:25:15,043
[SPEAKER_02]: So then, that's why, when the Buddha would talk, he would make a categorical difference.

306
00:25:15,223 --> 00:25:19,686
[SPEAKER_02]: He would say, no feeling, in case of an arahant, because there's no puthujjana's feeling.

307
00:25:20,107 --> 00:25:20,327
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

308
00:25:21,787 --> 00:25:23,828
[SPEAKER_02]: But that doesn't mean that it's just dead.

309
00:25:24,328 --> 00:25:27,089
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, again, that's another putujana's reference, then.

310
00:25:28,230 --> 00:25:31,071
[SPEAKER_02]: And none of the putujana's references can apply to an arahant.

311
00:25:32,391 --> 00:25:32,732
[SPEAKER_02]: Not even...

312
00:25:33,252 --> 00:25:41,695
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is why, as I said, if, you know, if putujana would understand what a state of arahant is, he would be on the way to become one.

313
00:25:42,756 --> 00:25:45,977
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's not that you can get a clear idea and still be a putujana.

314
00:25:46,477 --> 00:25:48,538
[SPEAKER_02]: Clear idea frees you from the state of putujana.

315
00:25:53,428 --> 00:25:55,689
[SPEAKER_00]: In the case of Āryas, it's a bit different.

316
00:25:55,989 --> 00:25:57,290
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, it's in between.

317
00:25:57,890 --> 00:26:03,593
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the overall picture, the fundamental principle, that's all understood, that's all set.

318
00:26:04,513 --> 00:26:13,257
[SPEAKER_02]: But they had to keep repeating it until all the habits, they were based on unwholesome greed, aversion, delusion, disappear.

319
00:26:13,957 --> 00:26:14,798
[SPEAKER_02]: And then they can stop.

320
00:26:18,930 --> 00:26:23,652
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you're trying to have like a clear definition how far, how much, you can't.

321
00:26:24,472 --> 00:26:36,096
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just... Well, the most precise you can get is what the Buddha himself used to describe the four different stages of enlightenment.

322
00:26:36,236 --> 00:26:37,637
[SPEAKER_02]: But in the beginning, they weren't.

323
00:26:38,960 --> 00:26:40,301
[SPEAKER_02]: He didn't... they didn't exist.

324
00:26:41,122 --> 00:26:43,684
[SPEAKER_02]: That doesn't mean the dhamma didn't exist, or the stages didn't exist.

325
00:26:44,064 --> 00:26:50,049
[SPEAKER_02]: It was basically somebody who understood the dhamma, and somebody who fully developed it, and now went beyond, and it's free.

326
00:26:52,011 --> 00:27:04,902
[SPEAKER_02]: And then there was that whole chapter in Samyutta Nikāvana, whenever somebody would die, Venerable Ananda would go to the Buddha and ask him, oh, Lord, such and such person died, what's their destination?

327
00:27:06,083 --> 00:27:07,184
[SPEAKER_02]: And then we say, oh, he was

328
00:27:07,735 --> 00:27:14,560
[SPEAKER_02]: He will return one more time, or he won't return at all, or he will be reborn in this heaven, he will be reborn...

329
00:27:14,580 --> 00:27:15,981
[SPEAKER_02]: But it was just becoming all the time.

330
00:27:16,001 --> 00:27:23,407
[SPEAKER_02]: And he said, well, if whenever somebody dies, you come and ask me about their rebirth, it's just going to be a real bother for me.

331
00:27:23,507 --> 00:27:32,374
[SPEAKER_02]: So, why don't I tell you something that you can use as a criteria to decide for yourself where most likely that person will be reborn.

332
00:27:32,935 --> 00:27:36,077
[SPEAKER_02]: So, Sotāpanna, at most, will have seven rebirths.

333
00:27:36,217 --> 00:27:36,918
[SPEAKER_02]: He might have less.

334
00:27:38,664 --> 00:27:42,765
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's not, ah, sotāpati, seven, or something like that.

335
00:27:42,845 --> 00:27:44,006
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a... it's a guideline.

336
00:27:44,206 --> 00:28:00,891
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a... it's like a little stopping point of one, actually, just one process, which is developing, understanding the dhamma, and fully developing that understanding.

337
00:28:01,071 --> 00:28:03,632
[SPEAKER_02]: So, then, you would say, sakadāgāmī would return one more time.

338
00:28:04,353 --> 00:28:05,533
[SPEAKER_02]: Anāgāmī wouldn't return.

339
00:28:06,942 --> 00:28:11,103
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, he would give that in relation to the ten fetters.

340
00:28:12,404 --> 00:28:19,146
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, by dwelling on those ten fetters, by contemplating them, you can fully understand them.

341
00:28:20,126 --> 00:28:22,847
[SPEAKER_02]: And when you fully understand them, you understand that you understood.

342
00:28:23,407 --> 00:28:25,308
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's not like, oh, have I understood?

343
00:28:25,348 --> 00:28:26,428
[SPEAKER_02]: It means, ah, there is doubt there.

344
00:28:26,448 --> 00:28:28,269
[SPEAKER_02]: It means you haven't understand them enough.

345
00:28:29,229 --> 00:28:31,190
[SPEAKER_02]: So, sometimes people might, when they get

346
00:28:33,457 --> 00:28:37,459
[SPEAKER_02]: When they overestimate themselves, or are just plain deluded, they refuse to doubt.

347
00:28:38,039 --> 00:28:40,040
[SPEAKER_02]: And they think that's the way to go beyond the doubt.

348
00:28:40,920 --> 00:28:42,521
[SPEAKER_02]: When they just do not doubt.

349
00:28:43,021 --> 00:28:45,082
[SPEAKER_02]: And if somebody doubts, they just ignore that.

350
00:28:45,882 --> 00:28:48,923
[SPEAKER_02]: If somebody tells them, no, I think, no, no, I refuse to accept that.

351
00:28:49,664 --> 00:28:51,044
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's, that's just ridiculous.

352
00:28:51,164 --> 00:28:52,725
[SPEAKER_02]: That doesn't, doesn't work like that.

353
00:28:53,045 --> 00:28:54,686
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you should doubt all the time.

354
00:28:56,486 --> 00:28:59,127
[SPEAKER_02]: Because doubt keeps turning things around.

355
00:29:00,328 --> 00:29:00,648
[SPEAKER_02]: And then,

356
00:29:01,780 --> 00:29:10,443
[SPEAKER_02]: It can make you, it can enable you to develop understanding that will then be truly impenetrable to your full-on acts of doubt.

357
00:29:11,744 --> 00:29:14,405
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if you never doubt, you don't know if you're beyond the doubt.

358
00:29:15,525 --> 00:29:24,689
[SPEAKER_02]: If you doubt all the time and learn how to, while doubting, go beyond that doubt, ah, then you can truly know for yourself and you cannot doubt that.

359
00:29:26,930 --> 00:29:29,391
[SPEAKER_02]: Because you're doubting and it makes no difference.

360
00:29:29,769 --> 00:29:33,371
[SPEAKER_00]: Lord Buddha encouraged people to question and doubt.

361
00:29:33,391 --> 00:29:34,452
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, yes.

362
00:29:34,732 --> 00:29:47,720
[SPEAKER_00]: Venerable Bhārata, you know, actually, not only once, several times, told me that Lord Buddha actually said that we must believe what he said.

363
00:29:48,786 --> 00:29:52,289
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you don't actually encourage people to question beliefs.

364
00:29:52,369 --> 00:29:53,029
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not correct.

365
00:29:53,129 --> 00:29:53,990
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I think.

366
00:29:54,010 --> 00:29:55,231
[SPEAKER_02]: No, you never... No, but that's it.

367
00:29:55,671 --> 00:29:59,534
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, not to question... Oh, yeah, he said, don't take my word for it.

368
00:29:59,554 --> 00:30:00,055
[SPEAKER_02]: He said that.

369
00:30:00,315 --> 00:30:02,016
[SPEAKER_02]: So, like, don't just believe what I say.

370
00:30:03,237 --> 00:30:07,520
[SPEAKER_02]: It's great for you if you have such faith, but you don't, don't, you don't have to.

371
00:30:07,660 --> 00:30:10,723
[SPEAKER_02]: No, like, you should examine it and see why is it so.

372
00:30:11,743 --> 00:30:14,746
[SPEAKER_02]: And then your faith will be unshakable, because you understood it.

373
00:30:17,169 --> 00:30:17,889
[SPEAKER_02]: Quite the opposite.

374
00:30:17,989 --> 00:30:18,629
[SPEAKER_02]: Quite the opposite.

375
00:30:18,649 --> 00:30:21,310
[SPEAKER_02]: Because often, yes, you would have... No, no, just don't doubt.

376
00:30:21,330 --> 00:30:22,451
[SPEAKER_02]: Just do what I say.

377
00:30:22,571 --> 00:30:23,291
[SPEAKER_02]: Stop asking.

378
00:30:23,771 --> 00:30:25,011
[SPEAKER_02]: No, no, it was like...

379
00:30:25,031 --> 00:30:28,813
[SPEAKER_02]: He would even encourage monks to keep interrogating each other all the time.

380
00:30:30,091 --> 00:30:31,352
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that's how you progress.

381
00:30:31,412 --> 00:30:32,933
[SPEAKER_02]: That's how you develop understanding.

382
00:30:34,254 --> 00:30:42,080
[SPEAKER_02]: Obviously, sometimes people can take that, oh, well, the Buddha encourages us to doubt and to question, and they mistake that for just being argumentative.

383
00:30:42,980 --> 00:30:48,904
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, when somebody just wants to argue over any little point, and then, well, the Buddha, well, no, he didn't say that.

384
00:30:48,944 --> 00:30:53,228
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, if you have a genuine interest, you have to keep overturning things and

385
00:30:54,279 --> 00:31:00,141
[SPEAKER_02]: Like digging, digging and digging and digging, whether through conversation with others or inside of your mind.

386
00:31:00,541 --> 00:31:03,802
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that's how understanding can come.

387
00:31:04,542 --> 00:31:16,565
[SPEAKER_02]: But, yeah, sometimes people just use that as an excuse to indulge their habits of arguments or just enjoying the debate or things like that.

388
00:31:17,245 --> 00:31:18,405
[SPEAKER_02]: And he didn't encourage that.

389
00:31:18,765 --> 00:31:20,726
[SPEAKER_02]: He didn't encourage debates for the sake of debates.

390
00:31:21,808 --> 00:31:25,710
[SPEAKER_00]: I think, in the history, this is actually, I really wanted to know it.

391
00:31:26,090 --> 00:31:34,795
[SPEAKER_00]: I think there are arias and arahants that, actually, even from the, even for the photojournalist, you can see clearly, like, it's perfect.

392
00:31:35,075 --> 00:31:45,201
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, actually, there are so many cases that in sutras, it's mentioned that there's some arias, like, it's like an immoral or whatever.

393
00:31:45,901 --> 00:31:51,224
[SPEAKER_00]: But I actually want, I was wondering this, arahants, some people thought he was angry.

394
00:31:51,755 --> 00:31:54,756
[SPEAKER_00]: But there's no anger in his mind.

395
00:31:55,136 --> 00:31:57,037
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

396
00:31:57,177 --> 00:31:57,677
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it?

397
00:31:57,737 --> 00:31:59,338
[SPEAKER_00]: How does it happen?

398
00:31:59,358 --> 00:32:05,701
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I heard like a very harsh speech and like a... Yeah, that was the case.

399
00:32:05,721 --> 00:32:11,543
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, first thing is, it's possible, but it's very unlikely.

400
00:32:13,473 --> 00:32:18,537
[SPEAKER_02]: So, when you get cases like that, there are a few cases in the suttas.

401
00:32:18,857 --> 00:32:26,643
[SPEAKER_02]: Or even Venerable Charnam and Sariputta, the one who committed suicide, that Sariputta thought that he wasn't an arahant.

402
00:32:27,584 --> 00:32:29,225
[SPEAKER_02]: And when Buddha said, well, why do you say that?

403
00:32:29,245 --> 00:32:34,590
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, oh, because he was associating with bad families, with families of bad reputations.

404
00:32:34,650 --> 00:32:36,111
[SPEAKER_02]: He was very close with them.

405
00:32:36,691 --> 00:32:38,813
[SPEAKER_02]: So they all thought bad of him.

406
00:32:39,513 --> 00:32:41,095
[SPEAKER_02]: But Buddha said, no, he was an arahant.

407
00:32:42,316 --> 00:32:45,562
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the external measure, it's not necessarily a measure.

408
00:32:46,624 --> 00:32:50,711
[SPEAKER_02]: And the putusiness criteria cannot encompass an arahant.

409
00:32:50,771 --> 00:32:52,174
[SPEAKER_02]: And if they do, it's accidentally.

410
00:32:52,454 --> 00:32:53,276
[SPEAKER_02]: They don't know why.

411
00:32:54,238 --> 00:32:55,079
[SPEAKER_02]: But, nevertheless,

412
00:32:56,322 --> 00:33:02,046
[SPEAKER_02]: For, I think, probably for 80 or 90 percent of people, arahant, it means there won't be any of those things left.

413
00:33:02,146 --> 00:33:14,175
[SPEAKER_02]: I think there is some... Well, like in the case of an angry, well, seemingly angry arahant, the Buddha said, oh, the reason why his speech is still like that is for 500 lifetimes.

414
00:33:14,395 --> 00:33:17,958
[SPEAKER_02]: 500 times he was reborn and lived, I don't know, a long time.

415
00:33:18,038 --> 00:33:21,020
[SPEAKER_02]: So, let's just say he lived 80 years each time.

416
00:33:21,220 --> 00:33:22,602
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like, oh, 4,000?

417
00:33:28,393 --> 00:33:42,457
[SPEAKER_02]: He was reborn in the Brahmin family and they were always higher than the others.

418
00:33:43,037 --> 00:33:45,158
[SPEAKER_02]: They would always speak condescendingly to the others.

419
00:33:46,076 --> 00:33:52,839
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the habit just got so established that even when he became an arahant, his aggregates just kept emulating the same form of speech.

420
00:33:53,120 --> 00:33:56,541
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you said that the feeling itself is different for the arahants.

421
00:33:56,581 --> 00:33:59,683
[SPEAKER_00]: It means anger really... Well, there's no anger, really.

422
00:34:00,043 --> 00:34:05,126
[SPEAKER_00]: I was thinking there was something, a kind of anger, but the thought itself was already disowned.

423
00:34:05,526 --> 00:34:11,969
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, again, the only way... the only way you can look at it is through your own... like...

424
00:34:12,668 --> 00:34:13,848
[SPEAKER_02]: Window of experience.

425
00:34:13,948 --> 00:34:16,489
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't really... And you can actually relate to that.

426
00:34:16,529 --> 00:34:26,133
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm sure everyone at some point experienced when some person told you something that you really believed it was like that, and then you realize it was completely different.

427
00:34:27,013 --> 00:34:31,215
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, while they were saying this, they meant something completely different.

428
00:34:31,855 --> 00:34:32,755
[SPEAKER_02]: And you didn't know that.

429
00:34:32,935 --> 00:34:33,916
[SPEAKER_02]: You couldn't know that.

430
00:34:35,176 --> 00:34:40,538
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in the same sense, when you come across somebody who speaks angrily, you end up assuming he's angry.

431
00:34:41,464 --> 00:34:44,966
[SPEAKER_02]: But then, you can find out later on that actually, no, it wasn't an overseas habit.

432
00:34:45,846 --> 00:34:49,988
[SPEAKER_02]: Or, if you go again, like, you know, I've been in Thailand, Sri Lanka, so on.

433
00:34:50,308 --> 00:34:53,209
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you see the different cultures, different things people do.

434
00:34:54,150 --> 00:34:58,472
[SPEAKER_02]: And sometimes, something, some things people do in Thailand, it's considered very rude in Europe.

435
00:34:59,372 --> 00:35:01,413
[SPEAKER_02]: And vice versa, something we do in Europe.

436
00:35:01,954 --> 00:35:08,497
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you know, there can be a different significance of the identical external output.

437
00:35:08,557 --> 00:35:08,817
[SPEAKER_02]: So,

438
00:35:09,617 --> 00:35:15,779
[SPEAKER_02]: You can do something exactly the same in one country, do exactly the same in another country, it can mean completely different.

439
00:35:16,380 --> 00:35:18,060
[SPEAKER_02]: Or even coming from the same person.

440
00:35:18,180 --> 00:35:21,722
[SPEAKER_02]: Sometimes you can say something exactly the same and be angry about it.

441
00:35:22,682 --> 00:35:25,663
[SPEAKER_02]: Sometimes you can say something exactly the same and not be angry about it.

442
00:35:26,203 --> 00:35:28,244
[SPEAKER_02]: So, anger is indifferent to that.

443
00:35:29,165 --> 00:35:32,026
[SPEAKER_02]: Often it's a manifestation of anger, i.e.

444
00:35:32,206 --> 00:35:33,866
[SPEAKER_02]: act of body, speech or mind.

445
00:35:34,747 --> 00:35:37,688
[SPEAKER_02]: But if there is no anger, there is still body, speech and mind.

446
00:35:38,117 --> 00:35:41,738
[SPEAKER_02]: And they will still be doing things, but it won't be coming from that angle.

447
00:35:44,719 --> 00:35:50,780
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, are you... is it... is it correct that I was thinking that the ariya means totally...

448
00:35:51,420 --> 00:35:56,981
[SPEAKER_00]: I think, like, become indifferent to the any feeling or thought or whatever it arises.

449
00:35:57,161 --> 00:36:03,803
[SPEAKER_00]: Actually, I was... that's why I was thinking that some of the, like, mis... misbehavior of arahanta... not arahanta, ariya,

450
00:36:04,302 --> 00:36:16,606
[SPEAKER_00]: I basically couldn't understand, because I think any kind of such unwholesome thought, whatever, in the case of ārya, not arahant, just then ārya means totally indifferent to such feelings.

451
00:36:16,626 --> 00:36:16,987
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, no.

452
00:36:17,027 --> 00:36:18,627
[SPEAKER_02]: Totally indifferent would be an arahant.

453
00:36:19,547 --> 00:36:20,008
[SPEAKER_00]: Different.

454
00:36:20,288 --> 00:36:22,128
[SPEAKER_02]: Totally indifferent would be an arahant.

455
00:36:22,328 --> 00:36:24,049
[SPEAKER_02]: Totally indifferent means equanimous.

456
00:36:24,069 --> 00:36:25,910
[SPEAKER_02]: That would be fully developed upekha.

457
00:36:26,882 --> 00:36:27,943
[SPEAKER_02]: That's totally different.

458
00:36:28,543 --> 00:36:33,766
[SPEAKER_02]: Forty thousand, by the way.

459
00:36:34,006 --> 00:36:40,069
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in case of an Ārya, again, it depends on how far his faculties developed.

460
00:36:42,050 --> 00:36:46,832
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you know, when... well, there was that sutta when the Buddha was staying in that forest.

461
00:36:48,773 --> 00:36:53,576
[SPEAKER_02]: And on the other side of that forest, there was a group of young men and women.

462
00:36:54,375 --> 00:37:09,182
[SPEAKER_02]: They paid those courtesans to come with them in the forest, so, you know, they were all getting loose and sensual, and then they all fell asleep after, and one of them woke up, one of the courtesans woke up, and stole all of their gold and money, and ran away.

463
00:37:09,917 --> 00:37:11,558
[SPEAKER_02]: When they realize, they start chasing her.

464
00:37:11,978 --> 00:37:22,321
[SPEAKER_02]: So, imagine their state of mind after this pleasure sensual evening and then full of anger over like, oh, we've been deceived and so on.

465
00:37:22,841 --> 00:37:24,882
[SPEAKER_02]: They were chasing to kill her, most likely.

466
00:37:26,062 --> 00:37:29,323
[SPEAKER_02]: And then they accidentally, as they were chasing, came across the Buddha.

467
00:37:29,363 --> 00:37:30,643
[SPEAKER_02]: They didn't even know he was the Buddha.

468
00:37:31,724 --> 00:37:35,805
[SPEAKER_02]: And so, he told them, well, why don't you sit down and I can teach you something instead.

469
00:37:36,025 --> 00:37:38,726
[SPEAKER_02]: It will be more beneficial to you than finding that gold.

470
00:37:40,109 --> 00:37:44,799
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you know, I'm sure some of them calmed down, but still...

471
00:37:44,879 --> 00:37:46,141
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm pretty sure they were still angry.

472
00:37:47,240 --> 00:37:48,801
[SPEAKER_02]: And Lenov said...

473
00:37:49,421 --> 00:37:53,403
[SPEAKER_02]: Yet, right there and then, he calmed them down.

474
00:37:53,483 --> 00:37:58,826
[SPEAKER_02]: First, he talked about the significance of virtue, why it's good to develop, and generosity.

475
00:37:59,427 --> 00:38:01,608
[SPEAKER_02]: They understood that, because everybody can understand that.

476
00:38:02,548 --> 00:38:06,450
[SPEAKER_02]: And that was a sufficient basis for them to then develop right view.

477
00:38:06,490 --> 00:38:09,552
[SPEAKER_02]: Because then he spoke about the dhamma, and they understood exactly what he meant.

478
00:38:10,052 --> 00:38:11,533
[SPEAKER_02]: And they all became sotāpānas.

479
00:38:12,954 --> 00:38:16,676
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you realize, oh, okay, it's not that hard then.

480
00:38:17,845 --> 00:38:25,507
[SPEAKER_02]: If somebody can have their mind full of lust, and then aversion, and anger, and then they could calm down, and then they could see the nature of things.

481
00:38:26,647 --> 00:38:28,548
[SPEAKER_02]: But that doesn't oblige them to follow it.

482
00:38:29,468 --> 00:38:32,109
[SPEAKER_02]: So, they might have well understood it, but...

483
00:38:32,129 --> 00:38:34,730
[SPEAKER_00]: I think it comes from their previous lives.

484
00:38:34,750 --> 00:38:42,352
[SPEAKER_02]: They have maybe practiced some... Well, I think what comes from previous life is the fact that you're born as human, with all of your faculties intact.

485
00:38:43,405 --> 00:38:47,387
[SPEAKER_02]: Nothing else excuses somebody for not understanding dhamma or understanding it.

486
00:38:48,027 --> 00:38:51,749
[SPEAKER_02]: In other words, those who make the effort will understand, those who don't, they won't.

487
00:38:53,149 --> 00:39:02,594
[SPEAKER_02]: But, yeah, there is sometimes previous lives, but I think people put too much emphasis on that as a way of absolving themselves from responsibility.

488
00:39:03,014 --> 00:39:03,934
[SPEAKER_02]: Ah, well, no.

489
00:39:04,074 --> 00:39:12,138
[SPEAKER_02]: If you are a born, intelligent, normal human being who can think normally, discern normally, there is no excuse for not understanding dhamma.

490
00:39:13,739 --> 00:39:14,359
[SPEAKER_02]: There just isn't.

491
00:39:14,599 --> 00:39:15,219
[SPEAKER_02]: Everything is there.

492
00:39:15,360 --> 00:39:19,681
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you don't understand, it's because you haven't been making the right effort.

493
00:39:20,281 --> 00:39:24,042
[SPEAKER_02]: And it's it's it's kind of blunt, but that's that's exactly how it is.

494
00:39:24,923 --> 00:39:34,646
[SPEAKER_02]: So, or when you had those, like, when sometimes the householders would come and see the Buddha and or even come to to complain about something or criticize him,

495
00:39:36,071 --> 00:39:40,334
[SPEAKER_02]: And then he would come and down, talk about dhamma, and then they would understand him and become sotāpannas.

496
00:39:40,874 --> 00:39:45,016
[SPEAKER_02]: And sometimes they would say, oh, from now on, I want to become a monk and practice this.

497
00:39:45,377 --> 00:39:46,257
[SPEAKER_02]: But sometimes they wouldn't.

498
00:39:46,277 --> 00:39:47,658
[SPEAKER_02]: Sometimes they would say, well, thank you, Lord.

499
00:39:48,238 --> 00:39:48,979
[SPEAKER_02]: We are very busy.

500
00:39:49,039 --> 00:39:50,120
[SPEAKER_02]: We have to go back home.

501
00:39:50,220 --> 00:39:51,360
[SPEAKER_02]: We have lots of things to attend to.

502
00:39:51,560 --> 00:39:52,261
[SPEAKER_02]: As sotāpannas.

503
00:39:52,581 --> 00:39:53,321
[SPEAKER_02]: Like his father.

504
00:39:53,922 --> 00:39:59,265
[SPEAKER_02]: When his father came and complained about Rākula, because he ordained him, he came all in tears and upset.

505
00:40:00,586 --> 00:40:04,108
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, first you went, now you took my only grandson.

506
00:40:04,248 --> 00:40:04,348
[SPEAKER_02]: But

507
00:40:07,867 --> 00:40:11,988
[SPEAKER_02]: Through explaining the dhamma, and then, oh, thank you very much, I could go now, I'm busy.

508
00:40:12,928 --> 00:40:21,291
[SPEAKER_02]: So, they don't necessarily always want to practice it, especially because, oh, this understanding is already quite an accomplishment, and they get content in that.

509
00:40:22,071 --> 00:40:25,353
[SPEAKER_02]: Which means that three quarters of their mind still needs to be

510
00:40:31,862 --> 00:40:32,362
[SPEAKER_02]: So to speak.

511
00:40:32,402 --> 00:40:40,646
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not that clear, clear-cut, but in the nutshell, they understood what needs to be done, but they don't necessarily want to do it right now.

512
00:40:41,626 --> 00:40:42,467
[SPEAKER_02]: They will eventually.

513
00:40:42,627 --> 00:40:43,948
[SPEAKER_02]: They can't ignore it for too long.

514
00:40:44,828 --> 00:40:47,990
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is why Sotāpanna said, at most, seven rebirths.

515
00:40:49,190 --> 00:40:49,890
[SPEAKER_02]: No more than that.

516
00:40:52,903 --> 00:41:02,569
[SPEAKER_02]: So that... and even... even that the whole, as I said before, the whole division of satipaṭṭhāna, sakadāgāmī, anāgāmī and arahant, sometimes the Buddha would omit sakadāgāmī.

517
00:41:04,250 --> 00:41:07,192
[SPEAKER_02]: He would just say satipaṭṭhāna and then he would start talking about anāgāmī.

518
00:41:08,793 --> 00:41:10,034
[SPEAKER_02]: And then he would talk about arahant.

519
00:41:11,095 --> 00:41:12,676
[SPEAKER_02]: And as I said in the beginning, there were no.

520
00:41:12,976 --> 00:41:15,738
[SPEAKER_02]: He was just fully enlightened or not fully enlightened.

521
00:41:17,579 --> 00:41:19,200
[SPEAKER_02]: So it's... it's a guideline.

522
00:41:20,012 --> 00:41:23,214
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a guideline, basically, of understanding the dhamma.

523
00:41:23,874 --> 00:41:26,395
[SPEAKER_02]: And it was also a guideline given to people to reassure them.

524
00:41:27,956 --> 00:41:38,301
[SPEAKER_02]: Just recently, I came again across that sutta of Mahānāma, the householder, who was... it wasn't clear in the suttas whether he was sakadāgāmī or sotāpāna, because he was never said.

525
00:41:38,321 --> 00:41:42,022
[SPEAKER_02]: It was always said that he's a noble disciple, who still lives the household life.

526
00:41:43,163 --> 00:41:46,064
[SPEAKER_02]: And there was this sutta when he says,

527
00:41:48,053 --> 00:41:57,220
[SPEAKER_02]: So, he knew that he was a... well, he knew that he was a noble disciple, but it was before the Buddha defined all of these seven rebirths or whatever.

528
00:41:57,240 --> 00:42:02,624
[SPEAKER_02]: So, he came to the Buddha one night, one evening, and he said, oh, how is it loaded?

529
00:42:03,124 --> 00:42:03,345
[SPEAKER_02]: Like,

530
00:42:04,178 --> 00:42:09,162
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, I'm the one who understood the dhamma, and I know that I understood it, and so on.

531
00:42:09,862 --> 00:42:29,036
[SPEAKER_02]: But on some occasions, when I walk on the... like now, when I come to see the Lord in the evening, and it's getting dark, and if I come across a stray dog, a stray man, or a stray elephant, or some form of danger, he forgets about all the dhamma he understood.

532
00:42:29,296 --> 00:42:30,917
[SPEAKER_02]: And, you know, he gets filled with fear.

533
00:42:32,063 --> 00:42:41,171
[SPEAKER_02]: And then he says, what if I were to die at that moment, on account of that stray dog, stray elephant, stray man, what will my destination be?

534
00:42:41,211 --> 00:42:52,660
[SPEAKER_02]: So, he was concerned that if he dies in that state of mind, although he knew that he was a noble disciple, he was concerned that if he dies in that state of mind, his destination might be unfortunate.

535
00:42:53,381 --> 00:42:56,804
[SPEAKER_02]: Because unless you have a supernatural power, you can't see those things.

536
00:42:58,333 --> 00:43:01,116
[SPEAKER_02]: And the Buddha said, oh, you have absolutely nothing to fear.

537
00:43:01,676 --> 00:43:06,860
[SPEAKER_02]: The one who understood the dhamma, who developed that kind of noble virtue, noble understanding, know what it is?

538
00:43:07,801 --> 00:43:13,546
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if your mind is overwhelmed with fear, when you die, it still won't make a difference to your destination.

539
00:43:16,168 --> 00:43:24,174
[SPEAKER_02]: But the interesting bit to me, then, is that, oh, there was a Sotāpanna or Sakadāgāmī, who wasn't sure about certain things.

540
00:43:25,035 --> 00:43:27,277
[SPEAKER_02]: He was sure about the nature of the dhamma, but he doesn't know

541
00:43:28,473 --> 00:43:35,139
[SPEAKER_02]: How far his understanding of that dhamma, the real understanding of the dhamma, how far takes him?

542
00:43:35,199 --> 00:43:51,894
[SPEAKER_02]: And for that, the Buddha would often talk like, I will see the mountain Himalayas and then he would pick up seven grains of sand and said, the size of that mountain is the size of suffering that Sotāpanna has abandoned and all he has left is the amount of this

543
00:43:53,578 --> 00:43:54,619
[SPEAKER_02]: Seven grains of sand.

544
00:43:54,739 --> 00:43:57,020
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's how much he has destroyed.

545
00:43:57,601 --> 00:44:01,523
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's quite an accomplishment even to get that understanding.

546
00:44:02,003 --> 00:44:04,645
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why he would give those encouragements of that kind.

547
00:44:04,705 --> 00:44:06,025
[SPEAKER_02]: But Sotāpanna doesn't see that.

548
00:44:06,085 --> 00:44:06,686
[SPEAKER_02]: He doesn't, you know...

549
00:44:06,706 --> 00:44:07,546
[SPEAKER_02]: He sees the dhamma.

550
00:44:07,566 --> 00:44:08,687
[SPEAKER_02]: He sees the timeless principle.

551
00:44:09,087 --> 00:44:10,628
[SPEAKER_02]: He sees the Four Noble Truths.

552
00:44:10,728 --> 00:44:11,929
[SPEAKER_02]: He sees the way out of suffering.

553
00:44:12,469 --> 00:44:14,550
[SPEAKER_02]: And actually, that in itself is more than enough.

554
00:44:15,571 --> 00:44:17,072
[SPEAKER_02]: All of this comes in as an extra.

555
00:44:18,913 --> 00:44:22,194
[SPEAKER_00]: All the arahants, they don't remember their previous life?

556
00:44:22,355 --> 00:44:22,535
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

557
00:44:23,576 --> 00:44:25,037
[SPEAKER_02]: Sariputta couldn't remember any of it.

558
00:44:26,118 --> 00:44:42,251
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, like, the foremost, after the Buddha, in teaching the dharma and expounding every angle and approach, just, you know, people couldn't see the difference whether he would speak or the Buddha, because he was just... he understood all of it.

559
00:44:42,971 --> 00:44:45,733
[SPEAKER_02]: But he said he couldn't see even a little goblin.

560
00:44:47,054 --> 00:44:47,655
[SPEAKER_02]: Like nothing.

561
00:44:47,675 --> 00:44:48,715
[SPEAKER_02]: He couldn't see any of that.

562
00:44:49,116 --> 00:44:50,437
[SPEAKER_02]: Neither supernatural nothing.

563
00:44:50,457 --> 00:44:51,738
[SPEAKER_02]: It was just non-existent for him.

564
00:44:52,744 --> 00:44:54,985
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is why it's irrelevant whether you see it or not.

565
00:44:55,305 --> 00:44:57,625
[SPEAKER_02]: It makes no difference.

566
00:44:57,805 --> 00:45:00,066
[SPEAKER_02]: Some people can, but...

567
00:45:00,106 --> 00:45:02,486
[SPEAKER_00]: I was thinking that remembering the past, right?

568
00:45:02,566 --> 00:45:04,707
[SPEAKER_00]: It's very important to understand the role of karma.

569
00:45:05,087 --> 00:45:05,907
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not necessary.

570
00:45:06,027 --> 00:45:06,627
[SPEAKER_02]: No, no, no.

571
00:45:06,667 --> 00:45:07,287
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, karma...

572
00:45:07,427 --> 00:45:12,609
[SPEAKER_02]: If karma is truly determined by your past, you're stuck.

573
00:45:13,309 --> 00:45:14,349
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't do anything about it.

574
00:45:15,589 --> 00:45:22,011
[SPEAKER_02]: If Angulimala's karma was in his past, he couldn't have not overcome it, because he couldn't bring those people back to life.

575
00:45:23,683 --> 00:45:23,883
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

576
00:45:24,523 --> 00:45:30,085
[SPEAKER_02]: Action is intention, and intention is very present thing, and things you do are very present, and that's where you abandon it.

577
00:45:31,386 --> 00:45:45,390
[SPEAKER_02]: And when you abandon your... through, like, fully understanding, through abandoning ignorance, when you abandon your action by body, speech or mind, nothing you have done, nothing you would have done, can pertain to you anymore.

578
00:45:46,071 --> 00:45:50,272
[SPEAKER_02]: In the same sense, when you abandon the nature of present, what's present,

579
00:45:50,882 --> 00:45:53,223
[SPEAKER_02]: You abandon past, that can be present.

580
00:45:53,303 --> 00:45:54,924
[SPEAKER_02]: You abandon future, that can be present.

581
00:45:55,405 --> 00:45:57,126
[SPEAKER_02]: And you abandon present, that's present.

582
00:45:57,406 --> 00:45:59,087
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no.

583
00:45:59,147 --> 00:46:00,607
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not, actually.

584
00:46:00,627 --> 00:46:00,888
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

585
00:46:00,908 --> 00:46:03,589
[SPEAKER_00]: I think it's important for them to understand vipāka.

586
00:46:09,265 --> 00:46:15,150
[SPEAKER_02]: Because karma, I think the whole notion got overly mystified now.

587
00:46:15,870 --> 00:46:23,316
[SPEAKER_02]: It almost becomes like this energy of of of righteousness, like, well, that's your karma, you deserve it, or things like that.

588
00:46:23,356 --> 00:46:25,798
[SPEAKER_02]: But karma means action.

589
00:46:28,600 --> 00:46:31,162
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, filling this cup with water is a form of karma.

590
00:46:34,240 --> 00:46:40,361
[SPEAKER_02]: Wholesome or unwholesome... People might want to dwell on vipāka, on the results of that kamma.

591
00:46:41,481 --> 00:46:45,402
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's something that can cause sense of urgency.

592
00:46:45,422 --> 00:46:53,004
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if you start thinking, well, like, you know, if I misbehave, if I do these things, I could end up experiencing very bad results.

593
00:46:54,064 --> 00:46:59,725
[SPEAKER_02]: And then that can inspire them to be more wholesome and do less evil or whatever they were doing before.

594
00:47:02,104 --> 00:47:12,133
[SPEAKER_02]: But in terms of thinking about karma and like understanding the workings of the karma or something like that, the Buddha said it can only bring madness or distraction.

595
00:47:13,454 --> 00:47:21,442
[SPEAKER_02]: Unless you're a Buddha, who can then fully understand the workings of the karma, if he directs his mind towards it,

596
00:47:22,718 --> 00:47:32,464
[SPEAKER_02]: If somebody else tries, you just end up distracting yourself about pointless things, or go mad on account of really trying to understand something that you cannot possibly comprehend.

597
00:47:34,425 --> 00:47:40,428
[SPEAKER_02]: Which, another, it just says it in a different way, that's irrelevant, in terms of understanding.

598
00:47:42,049 --> 00:47:45,791
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, you understand the nature of action, you abandon it.

599
00:47:45,831 --> 00:47:46,592
[SPEAKER_02]: But you don't understand

600
00:47:48,238 --> 00:47:55,741
[SPEAKER_02]: Why, when you've done something in the past, now that was the result of this... That's a complete, like, looking elsewhere in a wrong direction.

601
00:47:56,541 --> 00:48:00,422
[SPEAKER_00]: How would the Buddha teach the importance to be virtuous?

602
00:48:00,942 --> 00:48:03,343
[SPEAKER_00]: Virtue... How would he teach that?

603
00:48:03,443 --> 00:48:05,524
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, he would teach that because of the...

604
00:48:05,784 --> 00:48:08,105
[SPEAKER_02]: He would say about the vipāka, he would say about the results.

605
00:48:09,125 --> 00:48:13,987
[SPEAKER_02]: If you mean... And then, again, even then, he said, well, don't believe what I say, but reflect for yourself.

606
00:48:14,147 --> 00:48:17,108
[SPEAKER_02]: And I think Amāli said before, when he told those people,

607
00:48:18,176 --> 00:48:23,018
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, but it's a fact that you don't know whether there is a next life or not.

608
00:48:23,818 --> 00:48:24,739
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a matter of belief.

609
00:48:24,979 --> 00:48:26,179
[SPEAKER_02]: You might take it or you might not.

610
00:48:26,740 --> 00:48:31,542
[SPEAKER_02]: But actually, even then, you can use your intelligence.

611
00:48:31,562 --> 00:48:33,222
[SPEAKER_02]: So, even Pratyujana can understand that.

612
00:48:34,103 --> 00:48:41,446
[SPEAKER_02]: Whereby, he can just basically sit and think, okay, I don't know that there is a future life or not.

613
00:48:41,926 --> 00:48:44,547
[SPEAKER_02]: If I misbehave here, right now, in this life,

614
00:48:45,945 --> 00:48:47,887
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, I'll get in trouble from people.

615
00:48:48,748 --> 00:48:49,329
[SPEAKER_02]: That's a fact.

616
00:48:49,789 --> 00:48:50,650
[SPEAKER_02]: I'll be put in jail.

617
00:48:51,251 --> 00:48:52,012
[SPEAKER_02]: People will hate me.

618
00:48:52,072 --> 00:48:53,874
[SPEAKER_02]: If I get away with it, people won't trust me.

619
00:48:54,074 --> 00:48:54,774
[SPEAKER_02]: I have to be on the run.

620
00:48:54,795 --> 00:48:56,336
[SPEAKER_02]: I have to keep looking around all the time.

621
00:48:57,337 --> 00:48:58,518
[SPEAKER_02]: I'll live in fear, or whatever.

622
00:48:58,538 --> 00:49:00,020
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's all unwholesome states.

623
00:49:01,581 --> 00:49:07,588
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, if there is a future life, then he'll experience even worse rebirth on account of bad actions.

624
00:49:09,301 --> 00:49:15,884
[SPEAKER_02]: If there is no future life, okay, he won't experience any bad rebirth, but he'll still suffer in this life on account of his bad actions.

625
00:49:17,004 --> 00:49:24,267
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, the other person who thinks, well, if I do good in this life, well, people will praise me, which is nice.

626
00:49:25,068 --> 00:49:26,688
[SPEAKER_02]: People will be helpful to me, which is nice.

627
00:49:26,828 --> 00:49:29,670
[SPEAKER_02]: People will want to repay my kindness, which is nice.

628
00:49:30,430 --> 00:49:31,951
[SPEAKER_02]: People will trust me, which is nice.

629
00:49:32,966 --> 00:49:35,367
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, if there is no future life, well, so be it.

630
00:49:35,547 --> 00:49:37,469
[SPEAKER_02]: At least, I live a good life here.

631
00:49:38,249 --> 00:49:41,611
[SPEAKER_02]: If there is a future life, then I'll also get a better rebirth.

632
00:49:41,631 --> 00:49:42,731
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I win on both fronts.

633
00:49:43,232 --> 00:49:45,633
[SPEAKER_02]: And if I misbehave, I lose on both fronts.

634
00:49:46,454 --> 00:49:49,655
[SPEAKER_02]: So, even then, it doesn't have to be a matter of belief anymore.

635
00:49:50,056 --> 00:49:53,077
[SPEAKER_02]: There is a clear understanding and almost a practical.

636
00:49:53,878 --> 00:49:56,459
[SPEAKER_02]: And, oh, so it's actually... that's why this is wholesome.

637
00:49:56,799 --> 00:49:57,920
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why that's unwholesome.

638
00:49:58,660 --> 00:50:01,502
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why it's better to behave than to misbehave.

639
00:50:02,471 --> 00:50:07,817
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, they have no knowledge of previous lives, no knowledge of future, vipāka, waiting for them.

640
00:50:07,997 --> 00:50:12,542
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just intelligence, human intelligence, basic discerning.

641
00:50:13,503 --> 00:50:16,266
[SPEAKER_02]: You just stop and think for a second and it becomes apparent.

642
00:50:18,248 --> 00:50:22,792
[SPEAKER_02]: Because there's no denying that if you do unwholesome things, people will complain.

643
00:50:24,192 --> 00:50:30,455
[SPEAKER_00]: If you meet someone who can't stop doing wrong doing, how, how, how, how it can...

644
00:50:30,815 --> 00:50:37,718
[SPEAKER_00]: Some, some people, they're supposing in front of me, keep killing the snakes and all of those things, and it's impossible for them to stop.

645
00:50:37,818 --> 00:50:39,379
[SPEAKER_00]: They say it's impossible.

646
00:50:39,759 --> 00:50:40,960
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, they... Yeah.

647
00:50:40,980 --> 00:50:45,102
[SPEAKER_02]: How, how, how do you... No, they're lying, they're lying to themselves.

648
00:50:45,142 --> 00:50:50,885
[SPEAKER_02]: If they say it's impossible, means... What that means, doesn't mean it's impossible, means they can't be bothered to try to stop.

649
00:50:52,665 --> 00:50:53,586
[SPEAKER_02]: So they'll keep doing it.

650
00:50:54,448 --> 00:50:56,490
[SPEAKER_02]: But you can't stop it if they don't want to stop it.

651
00:50:57,790 --> 00:51:01,553
[SPEAKER_02]: Unless you're stronger than them and twist their arm and restrain them until the snake escapes.

652
00:51:02,273 --> 00:51:02,854
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't do it.

653
00:51:03,754 --> 00:51:04,195
[SPEAKER_02]: So...

654
00:51:04,295 --> 00:51:05,936
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you never give any advice?

655
00:51:06,556 --> 00:51:07,157
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, there isn't.

656
00:51:07,237 --> 00:51:07,977
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, you could.

657
00:51:08,578 --> 00:51:10,019
[SPEAKER_02]: Again, you can...

658
00:51:10,539 --> 00:51:15,682
[SPEAKER_02]: If you try to convince people too hard, they will start ignoring you even harder.

659
00:51:17,644 --> 00:51:22,027
[SPEAKER_02]: So, unless the person who's just done something silly, unwholesome in front of you,

660
00:51:23,049 --> 00:51:24,670
[SPEAKER_02]: He's not willing to listen to you.

661
00:51:25,090 --> 00:51:27,931
[SPEAKER_02]: No amount of things you say, and you'll be wasting your words then.

662
00:51:28,711 --> 00:51:29,932
[SPEAKER_02]: They won't take any heed.

663
00:51:31,092 --> 00:51:37,015
[SPEAKER_02]: If they come and, oh, yeah, okay, well, like, if they really want to understand, then it's a different matter.

664
00:51:37,115 --> 00:51:39,036
[SPEAKER_02]: And then you can tell them just what I told you now.

665
00:51:40,256 --> 00:51:49,980
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, even if you don't know whether your bad action will bring the future misery in terms of the future rebirth,

666
00:51:50,755 --> 00:51:54,177
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you can still know for sure that in this life it will.

667
00:51:55,178 --> 00:51:59,401
[SPEAKER_02]: And also, the fact is that you don't know whether you're going to be reborn or not.

668
00:52:00,062 --> 00:52:04,725
[SPEAKER_02]: Surely, you should play it safe, as if you are going to be reborn.

669
00:52:06,526 --> 00:52:09,168
[SPEAKER_02]: So, just in case, I'll behave here.

670
00:52:09,848 --> 00:52:11,349
[SPEAKER_02]: I'll be praised here on my behavior.

671
00:52:12,010 --> 00:52:15,212
[SPEAKER_02]: And, if there is a future rebirth, then I'll be safe.

672
00:52:16,333 --> 00:52:18,855
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's almost like a precaution, just in case I'll behave.

673
00:52:19,789 --> 00:52:22,851
[SPEAKER_02]: If I know for sure that there is no life, this is the only life, yeah, I'll do whatever I like.

674
00:52:22,891 --> 00:52:24,311
[SPEAKER_02]: But you cannot know that for sure.

675
00:52:25,412 --> 00:52:26,652
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's something you can know.

676
00:52:27,553 --> 00:52:31,255
[SPEAKER_02]: You can know for sure that you don't know these things for sure, and you can never will.

677
00:52:31,835 --> 00:52:37,378
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is why it's better to, you know, use wisdom and say, well, why would I, like, risk?

678
00:52:38,218 --> 00:52:38,638
[SPEAKER_02]: There's no...

679
00:52:38,858 --> 00:52:41,500
[SPEAKER_02]: Especially when we're not risking, i.e.

680
00:52:41,700 --> 00:52:48,223
[SPEAKER_02]: behaving properly, will earn me respect in this life, and protect me in the future life, if there is one,

681
00:52:49,405 --> 00:52:50,806
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a winner in every respect.

682
00:52:51,606 --> 00:52:54,288
[SPEAKER_02]: But people usually say, well, I don't know that there is a rebirth.

683
00:52:54,748 --> 00:52:57,149
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, yeah, but you don't know that there isn't a rebirth either.

684
00:52:57,890 --> 00:52:58,970
[SPEAKER_02]: But they don't think that far.

685
00:52:58,990 --> 00:53:05,274
[SPEAKER_02]: They just think the first part, because that means, oh, well, I don't believe that I can do what I want here.

686
00:53:05,954 --> 00:53:11,698
[SPEAKER_02]: But actually, even if you don't believe or believe, if you stop and think, it will become apparent why you shouldn't be doing those things.

687
00:53:13,119 --> 00:53:16,502
[SPEAKER_02]: But, again, unless a person is willing to hear that, he won't listen to you.

688
00:53:16,942 --> 00:53:19,104
[SPEAKER_00]: He won't listen to anybody.

689
00:53:19,244 --> 00:53:26,690
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, yes, sometimes people come and ask him the wrong question, or they would formulate the question wrongly.

690
00:53:27,610 --> 00:53:41,561
[SPEAKER_02]: He said that sometimes Tathāgata meets this person and Tathāgata has inclination to teach him, but then this misguided person formulates his question wrongly, and then Tathāgata goes silent.

691
00:53:43,354 --> 00:53:44,375
[SPEAKER_02]: And he doesn't want to teach him that.

692
00:53:45,756 --> 00:53:47,898
[SPEAKER_02]: Because they came from a wrong place.

693
00:53:48,938 --> 00:53:50,840
[SPEAKER_02]: Because whatever he says, they'll take it wrongly.

694
00:53:51,440 --> 00:53:57,525
[SPEAKER_02]: Or sometimes, they come with a question and ask him, like when they ask him, oh, Master Gautama, is there a self?

695
00:54:00,067 --> 00:54:04,791
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you say, yes, there is, there isn't, that person will take that not-self as self.

696
00:54:05,531 --> 00:54:08,654
[SPEAKER_02]: Because he already implied in his question that there is a self.

697
00:54:08,694 --> 00:54:09,534
[SPEAKER_02]: So, which one is it?

698
00:54:10,790 --> 00:54:11,750
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the Buddha was quiet.

699
00:54:11,770 --> 00:54:15,592
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, well, does that mean there isn't, or there is, or isn't, or both, or neither?

700
00:54:16,352 --> 00:54:17,232
[SPEAKER_02]: And he was just quiet.

701
00:54:17,712 --> 00:54:18,673
[SPEAKER_02]: And then this person left.

702
00:54:19,493 --> 00:54:22,274
[SPEAKER_02]: And Ānanda, Venerable Ānanda asked him, well, why didn't you answer?

703
00:54:22,294 --> 00:54:26,536
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, well, because whatever I would have said, he would have misunderstood.

704
00:54:27,156 --> 00:54:27,376
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

705
00:54:27,416 --> 00:54:29,757
[SPEAKER_02]: Because he was coming with a completely wrong attitude.

706
00:54:30,677 --> 00:54:32,138
[SPEAKER_02]: So, he wasn't willing to understand.

707
00:54:33,018 --> 00:54:38,420
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, I think, on a later occasion, when Ānanda told him, you should ask like this, not, don't ask like that,

708
00:54:39,066 --> 00:54:42,728
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, when he asked rightly, the Tathāgata taught him, and then he understood.

709
00:54:43,729 --> 00:54:51,073
[SPEAKER_00]: It was actually a big surprise, before the... Venerable Garapānyu, he first time told me that there is no self is wrong.

710
00:54:51,693 --> 00:54:52,434
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no self.

711
00:54:52,454 --> 00:54:56,936
[SPEAKER_00]: Before, always I heard that all monks, they keep, there is no self, there is no self.

712
00:54:56,976 --> 00:54:57,937
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, it's a denial.

713
00:54:58,177 --> 00:54:59,398
[SPEAKER_00]: It is quite...

714
00:54:59,478 --> 00:55:01,359
[SPEAKER_00]: I actually didn't notice my mistake.

715
00:55:01,419 --> 00:55:06,081
[SPEAKER_00]: And he told, he told me that in the case of the general, it's impossible to say that there is no self.

716
00:55:06,202 --> 00:55:06,682
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

717
00:55:07,315 --> 00:55:09,516
[SPEAKER_00]: But why many monks they don't know in Sīla?

718
00:55:09,536 --> 00:55:10,636
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, because they are putujanas.

719
00:55:12,056 --> 00:55:13,537
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, maybe.

720
00:55:13,677 --> 00:55:17,298
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, if somebody's not a putujana, they wouldn't be saying that.

721
00:55:17,398 --> 00:55:21,399
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not written in sutra that for putujana we can't say that there is no self.

722
00:55:22,179 --> 00:55:23,479
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing written in sutra.

723
00:55:23,799 --> 00:55:24,320
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing.

724
00:55:24,760 --> 00:55:31,702
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, putujana should see through the nature of his self that it cannot be his, and that's why it becomes not-self.

725
00:55:32,582 --> 00:55:33,802
[SPEAKER_02]: It doesn't mean not-self.

726
00:55:34,622 --> 00:55:35,383
[SPEAKER_02]: It means not-self.

727
00:55:35,423 --> 00:55:36,523
[SPEAKER_02]: It means not-myself.

728
00:55:38,820 --> 00:55:40,661
[SPEAKER_02]: I think that's... that's not just... that's everywhere.

729
00:55:40,841 --> 00:55:42,221
[SPEAKER_02]: In the West, in Thailand, everywhere.

730
00:55:42,561 --> 00:55:43,722
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not just in Sri Lanka.

731
00:55:44,202 --> 00:55:48,743
[SPEAKER_02]: Because when anybody comes across Buddhism on the superficial terms, that's the first thing you come across.

732
00:55:48,783 --> 00:55:49,744
[SPEAKER_02]: Ah, there is no self.

733
00:55:51,224 --> 00:55:53,365
[SPEAKER_02]: And then that negative, like, becomes your self.

734
00:55:54,745 --> 00:55:56,285
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, yes, I don't believe in self.

735
00:55:56,566 --> 00:55:57,566
[SPEAKER_02]: That's my self-belief.

736
00:55:59,646 --> 00:56:00,807
[SPEAKER_02]: It's much harder than that.

737
00:56:02,300 --> 00:56:03,381
[SPEAKER_02]: As I said before, no.

738
00:56:03,521 --> 00:56:10,146
[SPEAKER_02]: You actually have to first see what your self is, in order to stop regarding it as yours.

739
00:56:10,726 --> 00:56:11,867
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's a very different thing.

740
00:56:12,287 --> 00:56:12,407
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

741
00:56:12,587 --> 00:56:15,309
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's why you have atta, and then you have anatta.

742
00:56:15,489 --> 00:56:18,731
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, not no-self, but not-self.

743
00:56:19,012 --> 00:56:19,412
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

744
00:56:19,830 --> 00:56:23,951
[SPEAKER_02]: But that which is taken as self, and then understood as not-self, is still there.

745
00:56:24,611 --> 00:56:26,072
[SPEAKER_02]: That thing doesn't change.

746
00:56:26,092 --> 00:56:28,732
[SPEAKER_02]: But when you say no-self, you just deny the whole thing.

747
00:56:29,032 --> 00:56:31,353
[SPEAKER_00]: In foreign, there is self, but it's not self.

748
00:56:31,393 --> 00:56:34,034
[SPEAKER_02]: There is that, which used to be taken as self.

749
00:56:34,074 --> 00:56:34,634
[SPEAKER_00]: Used to be.

750
00:56:34,694 --> 00:56:34,894
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

751
00:56:34,934 --> 00:56:35,954
[SPEAKER_02]: But now there is no-self.

752
00:56:36,174 --> 00:56:36,594
[SPEAKER_00]: No-self.

753
00:56:36,914 --> 00:56:38,475
[SPEAKER_02]: Or rather, it's not-self.

754
00:56:38,495 --> 00:56:38,895
[SPEAKER_00]: Not-self.

755
00:56:39,095 --> 00:56:41,275
[SPEAKER_02]: Every experience of an Arahant is not-self.

756
00:56:41,295 --> 00:56:41,736
[SPEAKER_02]: Not-self.

757
00:56:43,616 --> 00:56:47,237
[SPEAKER_00]: What does it mean that eight putkarā and then one puttu, puttu jhāna?

758
00:56:48,250 --> 00:56:52,051
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry?

759
00:56:52,391 --> 00:56:53,772
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, it means what I said before.

760
00:56:53,852 --> 00:56:55,752
[SPEAKER_02]: It's... it's only... it was only defined later.

761
00:56:56,872 --> 00:57:08,176
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the commentary and the interpretation of those mind moments, when you kind of want to and then you enter into the... you enter the path, then you realize the fruit, the next moment is... it's just lots of nonsense.

762
00:57:09,786 --> 00:57:20,812
[SPEAKER_02]: In the suttas means... And again, even that division came from the Buddha, from somebody who had such vision, but people might not know that necessarily for themselves, or for others.

763
00:57:21,633 --> 00:57:23,374
[SPEAKER_02]: When Sariputra couldn't recognize another ant.

764
00:57:24,034 --> 00:57:28,637
[SPEAKER_02]: There is another sutta, I think, I might have mentioned before, about Venerable Padiya, wasn't it?

765
00:57:28,857 --> 00:57:29,357
[SPEAKER_02]: The Dwarf.

766
00:57:30,238 --> 00:57:31,098
[SPEAKER_02]: You've been mocked at.

767
00:57:31,518 --> 00:57:32,039
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, no, no.

768
00:57:32,059 --> 00:57:33,259
[SPEAKER_02]: You told me that.

769
00:57:33,279 --> 00:57:34,120
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

770
00:57:34,200 --> 00:57:37,942
[SPEAKER_02]: In Udanas, when Sariputra was teaching this monk,

771
00:57:39,465 --> 00:57:41,507
[SPEAKER_02]: So he actually didn't have any work to do anymore.

772
00:57:41,527 --> 00:57:42,568
[SPEAKER_02]: But that wasn't apparent.

773
00:58:01,879 --> 00:58:06,981
[SPEAKER_00]: In sutra, clearly, it's written that eight, eight pūkarā, one pūkarā.

774
00:58:07,001 --> 00:58:07,742
[SPEAKER_00]: What does it mean?

775
00:58:07,782 --> 00:58:13,764
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, no, it's clearly written in terms of, again, as it's clearly written that there are, like, four stages of enlightenment.

776
00:58:13,805 --> 00:58:14,925
[SPEAKER_02]: The Buddha talked about it.

777
00:58:15,305 --> 00:58:29,852
[SPEAKER_02]: The Buddha said that there is a somebody, there is a difference, like, within, within somebody who is a sotāpanna, there is a difference between somebody who fully realized that he's a sotāpanna and somebody who didn't quite yet realize that he's actually a sotāpanna.

778
00:58:31,215 --> 00:58:35,659
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in other words, you develop understanding of the Four Noble Truths, for example.

779
00:58:38,641 --> 00:58:39,442
[SPEAKER_02]: And you understood it.

780
00:58:40,302 --> 00:58:44,386
[SPEAKER_02]: But now, the benefits of that understanding have not become apparent yet.

781
00:58:46,127 --> 00:58:48,629
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the difference between the path and the fruit.

782
00:58:51,151 --> 00:58:57,516
[SPEAKER_02]: For some people, they understood it, and they understood it so hard, that the benefits are pretty much immediate.

783
00:58:58,137 --> 00:58:59,178
[SPEAKER_02]: For others, they are not.

784
00:59:00,628 --> 00:59:13,476
[SPEAKER_02]: But what the Buddha said in relation to that is that if somebody has understood, like in case of Sotāpanna, somebody understood the dhamma, the benefits will have to be reached, if not before, then at the moment of its death.

785
00:59:16,078 --> 00:59:20,620
[SPEAKER_02]: And it's not... nothing kind of magical or mystical.

786
00:59:21,201 --> 00:59:27,405
[SPEAKER_02]: It simply means that nature of understanding of the dhamma basically goes through the nature of death.

787
00:59:31,002 --> 00:59:40,350
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you understood that, when those aggregates do start falling apart, you will fall apart through that understanding.

788
00:59:40,451 --> 00:59:41,792
[SPEAKER_02]: Because that's what you have understood.

789
00:59:42,252 --> 00:59:47,497
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is why, at the moment of death, the latest, you will get the benefits of your understanding.

790
00:59:49,350 --> 00:59:52,051
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the same for for any of the Arahants.

791
00:59:52,211 --> 00:59:58,653
[SPEAKER_02]: Whether it's a Sotāpanna and his fruit, Sakadāgāmī and his fruit, or Anāgāmī and his fruit, or Arahant and his fruit.

792
00:59:59,474 --> 01:00:03,395
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a clear line between Anāgāmī and Arahant, or Sakadāgāmī.

793
01:00:03,455 --> 01:00:04,335
[SPEAKER_00]: No, there isn't.

794
01:00:04,515 --> 01:00:05,936
[SPEAKER_02]: No, there isn't a clear line at all.

795
01:00:06,056 --> 01:00:12,058
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why there's that Sutta when all those monks came, I don't know, hundreds of them, and they all declared Arahantship.

796
01:00:14,045 --> 01:00:16,326
[SPEAKER_02]: And somebody asked the Buddha, is that really the case?

797
01:00:16,346 --> 01:00:22,167
[SPEAKER_02]: And he said, some of them are truly arahants, but some of them overestimated themselves and they're still anāgāmīs.

798
01:00:23,387 --> 01:00:28,248
[SPEAKER_02]: So, he didn't like, you know, they were not like putujanas, completely deluded, thinking they're arahants.

799
01:00:28,288 --> 01:00:30,509
[SPEAKER_02]: No, they were anāgāmīs, thinking they were arahants.

800
01:00:32,229 --> 01:00:34,090
[SPEAKER_00]: He could distinguish it.

801
01:00:34,150 --> 01:00:34,390
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

802
01:00:35,796 --> 01:00:39,179
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for Lord Buddha, there's a clear line between anāgāmī and ānāgāmī?

803
01:00:39,479 --> 01:00:39,699
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

804
01:00:40,140 --> 01:00:40,340
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

805
01:00:40,400 --> 01:00:43,402
[SPEAKER_02]: Because he was the Sāma, Sambuddha.

806
01:00:43,883 --> 01:00:44,543
[SPEAKER_00]: Only he can.

807
01:00:45,104 --> 01:00:51,329
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, only he can, or sometimes people who had ability to see other people's minds, with a special ability, they could see.

808
01:00:52,670 --> 01:00:55,592
[SPEAKER_02]: But that was, you know, random.

809
01:00:55,672 --> 01:00:56,593
[SPEAKER_02]: There was not a guarantee.

810
01:00:58,162 --> 01:01:00,603
[SPEAKER_00]: So, so, so that is the meaning of eight-pākara.

811
01:01:00,643 --> 01:01:02,764
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, the line is not clear.

812
01:01:03,325 --> 01:01:07,147
[SPEAKER_02]: It's, it's basically, as I said, it's a, it's a one development.

813
01:01:07,387 --> 01:01:12,629
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why somebody can become an arahant in the first watch of the night.

814
01:01:13,089 --> 01:01:15,791
[SPEAKER_02]: But it doesn't mean... it's the same path they walk.

815
01:01:16,371 --> 01:01:21,934
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just how fast they walk it and whether they stop at the resting stops or not.

816
01:01:23,762 --> 01:01:24,422
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the difference.

817
01:01:24,462 --> 01:01:25,543
[SPEAKER_02]: But it's one path.

818
01:01:26,303 --> 01:01:27,143
[SPEAKER_02]: It's the same path.

819
01:01:27,243 --> 01:01:32,045
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not like trying to achieve four different things or something like that.

820
01:01:32,725 --> 01:01:36,266
[SPEAKER_02]: And even those four different things are sometimes three different things.

821
01:01:36,286 --> 01:01:38,667
[SPEAKER_02]: When he omitted sakadāgāmī from the descriptions.

822
01:01:39,988 --> 01:01:45,349
[SPEAKER_02]: Or if you read in the Sānyūtanikaya about... like you think, okay, anāgāmī means rebirth in pure abodes.

823
01:01:46,450 --> 01:01:49,171
[SPEAKER_02]: But there is a whole chapter in Sānyūtanikaya about anāgāmīs.

824
01:01:50,911 --> 01:01:52,752
[SPEAKER_02]: There are like hundreds of different

825
01:01:54,048 --> 01:02:02,617
[SPEAKER_02]: realms within the pure abodes, whereby the anāgāmīs would be reborn, depending on how big anāgāmīs they were.

826
01:02:03,898 --> 01:02:08,183
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's just like... it's... it's... it's... well, it's kind of irrelevant in that sense.

827
01:02:08,443 --> 01:02:15,491
[SPEAKER_02]: It's relevant in a sense of abandoning the ten fetters, and that's something some people can contemplate and practice in regard to.

828
01:02:17,920 --> 01:02:27,248
[SPEAKER_02]: How far and what rebirth, unless there's supernatural powers or unless you live with the Buddha or somebody like that, it will be a distraction.

829
01:02:31,031 --> 01:02:34,994
[SPEAKER_02]: And it's not, it's not, it's not directly related to the practice of the dhamma.

830
01:02:35,914 --> 01:02:39,918
[SPEAKER_02]: It can be an encouragement, if it comes from... Well, again, you don't need to have somebody now.

831
01:02:39,938 --> 01:02:44,902
[SPEAKER_02]: If you read the suttas, you'll see what the Buddha was saying in relation to people who attained this or this or this.

832
01:02:52,396 --> 01:02:56,420
[SPEAKER_00]: I want to know that... what did you mean?

833
01:02:56,440 --> 01:03:03,666
[SPEAKER_00]: You said that what I see, Babalu, in the mind, you said that that is the thought, thought of sound, the thought of sight.

834
01:03:04,046 --> 01:03:04,867
[SPEAKER_00]: What does it mean?

835
01:03:04,887 --> 01:03:05,388
[SPEAKER_00]: Or a thought?

836
01:03:05,408 --> 01:03:09,751
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, five senses are known for what they are because of your mind.

837
01:03:10,552 --> 01:03:11,713
[SPEAKER_02]: They all meet in your mind.

838
01:03:13,515 --> 01:03:16,277
[SPEAKER_02]: And because of that, the mind comes first.

839
01:03:16,538 --> 01:03:16,778
[SPEAKER_02]: Like the

840
01:03:17,851 --> 01:03:24,233
[SPEAKER_02]: Because in the first verse of Dhammapada, the mind is the forerunner of all things.

841
01:03:24,253 --> 01:03:39,339
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in other words, if you, like, if you have an object of your sight, again, if you look at a flask in front of you, see, you can't just have a pure sight, because even colors are distinguished as different colors.

842
01:03:43,707 --> 01:03:49,450
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, your eyes cannot distinguish... like, if your eyes would operate without your mind, it would... well, it's inconceivable.

843
01:03:49,670 --> 01:03:50,651
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't even think like that.

844
01:03:51,011 --> 01:03:54,853
[SPEAKER_02]: In other words, when you look at the flask, there is already a significance implied in it.

845
01:03:54,913 --> 01:03:56,754
[SPEAKER_02]: There is already a thought of flask.

846
01:03:57,455 --> 01:04:05,479
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if you know... don't know the name, don't know what it is, there is a thought of object being seen.

847
01:04:06,600 --> 01:04:07,380
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's the sight.

848
01:04:13,528 --> 01:04:25,110
[SPEAKER_00]: Before... before I heard, eh... just after the... with consciousness, the external object, an internal sense organ, with the help of consciousness, the contact arises.

849
01:04:25,570 --> 01:04:27,731
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, that sight is called feeling.

850
01:04:28,071 --> 01:04:28,891
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not correct.

851
01:04:28,911 --> 01:04:31,612
[SPEAKER_00]: The perception means that this... this is... Yeah.

852
01:04:31,652 --> 01:04:33,012
[SPEAKER_02]: Perception means perception.

853
01:04:33,052 --> 01:04:33,252
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

854
01:04:34,212 --> 01:04:34,712
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

855
01:04:35,132 --> 01:04:36,473
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't feel your perception.

856
01:04:36,773 --> 01:04:37,733
[SPEAKER_02]: You feel your feeling.

857
01:04:38,693 --> 01:04:40,614
[SPEAKER_00]: Feeling... what... suppose it depends.

858
01:04:40,634 --> 01:04:41,714
[SPEAKER_00]: Say, I look at this flask.

859
01:04:41,774 --> 01:04:42,554
[SPEAKER_00]: What kind of feeling?

860
01:04:43,177 --> 01:04:43,757
[SPEAKER_02]: Maybe nothing.

861
01:04:44,658 --> 01:04:45,579
[SPEAKER_02]: Maybe neutral feeling.

862
01:04:46,860 --> 01:04:58,929
[SPEAKER_02]: But if it's perhaps something, I don't know, something very significant that some person gave you, and if you look at that, then the feeling will arise.

863
01:05:00,910 --> 01:05:07,736
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it doesn't always... That's the thing, like, feeling has its own domain, perception has its own domain.

864
01:05:11,432 --> 01:05:18,200
[SPEAKER_02]: If you want to understand it correctly, you have to start applying that understanding to those domains correctly.

865
01:05:18,260 --> 01:05:21,584
[SPEAKER_02]: In other words, if I ask you, how do you feel now?

866
01:05:22,284 --> 01:05:23,506
[SPEAKER_02]: There has to be a feeling there.

867
01:05:24,267 --> 01:05:27,170
[SPEAKER_02]: And you can say, oh, I feel all right, which is a form of pleasant.

868
01:05:27,230 --> 01:05:29,893
[SPEAKER_02]: You can say, well, I don't feel that well, I'm a bit tired, which is a form of unpleasant.

869
01:05:29,913 --> 01:05:31,295
[SPEAKER_02]: You can say, I feel neutral.

870
01:05:33,842 --> 01:05:45,391
[SPEAKER_02]: And that situation, a general situation of you sitting here in this kuti, talking on dhamma, that is already something that you're perceiving, peripherally, in your thoughts.

871
01:05:46,652 --> 01:05:48,854
[SPEAKER_02]: It's present, because if I ask you, where are you now?

872
01:05:49,354 --> 01:05:50,975
[SPEAKER_02]: You will immediately know where you are.

873
01:05:51,035 --> 01:05:56,800
[SPEAKER_02]: You won't have to stop and figure out, because it's already there, so you just have to access it with your attention.

874
01:05:59,064 --> 01:06:03,428
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, if you forget about that, which is where actually, where it should start.

875
01:06:03,589 --> 01:06:07,092
[SPEAKER_02]: The contemplation has to start at the most general level you're capable of.

876
01:06:08,413 --> 01:06:19,064
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you don't know that, then you will take a random object and try to examine it, almost scientifically or psychologically, and then try to discover the nature of perception and nature of feeling.

877
01:06:19,104 --> 01:06:21,387
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I look at this thing in my hands.

878
01:06:21,567 --> 01:06:22,488
[SPEAKER_02]: What do I feel?

879
01:06:23,917 --> 01:06:28,681
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if you gain any understanding from that, it will be on the level of intellectual satisfaction.

880
01:06:28,841 --> 01:06:29,822
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, yes, it makes sense.

881
01:06:30,703 --> 01:06:36,167
[SPEAKER_02]: But leaves the whole domain of feeling and perception on the general level absolutely intact.

882
01:06:38,389 --> 01:06:42,913
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in other words, you have to start from the opposite direction.

883
01:06:42,973 --> 01:06:51,160
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you have to think you are here, away from home, in this kuti, talking about dhamma.

884
01:06:52,429 --> 01:06:54,030
[SPEAKER_02]: Or you can define it whichever way you are.

885
01:06:54,791 --> 01:06:56,452
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's your current situation.

886
01:06:57,812 --> 01:07:01,695
[SPEAKER_02]: Within that situation, there are objects around you.

887
01:07:01,955 --> 01:07:12,762
[SPEAKER_02]: Within those objects, there are more particular objects, there are more important objects, there are less important objects, there are more useful objects, like that cushion, there are less useful objects, like that bed, that you don't need anymore.

888
01:07:13,903 --> 01:07:19,166
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's all within that experience as a whole of you being in this room, sitting down and talking.

889
01:07:20,550 --> 01:07:24,193
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that was that the mindfulness of the body postures works on that level.

890
01:07:25,474 --> 01:07:32,140
[SPEAKER_02]: If you're aware of your body posture, that's almost like the most general thing that determines your current situation.

891
01:07:33,561 --> 01:07:39,526
[SPEAKER_02]: And that situation is perceived, that situation is felt, that situation is intended.

892
01:07:46,515 --> 01:07:50,036
[SPEAKER_02]: On the level of that situation is where the contact happens.

893
01:07:50,056 --> 01:07:58,058
[SPEAKER_02]: And only because you're already contacted, you can distinguish all these particular things around you, with your eyes, ears, nose, and so on.

894
01:08:02,779 --> 01:08:07,180
[SPEAKER_02]: So, on that general situation, is where the three meet.

895
01:08:08,180 --> 01:08:08,940
[SPEAKER_02]: But they don't meet like

896
01:08:12,444 --> 01:08:13,985
[SPEAKER_02]: Cause and effect.

897
01:08:14,125 --> 01:08:25,952
[SPEAKER_02]: As often the commentaries interpret, we say you have one hand, another hand, you have the sense organs, and the consciousness, and they meet, and the sound is the sound, or whatever it was.

898
01:08:25,972 --> 01:08:29,255
[SPEAKER_02]: No, the sound is the contact, doesn't it?

899
01:08:30,095 --> 01:08:31,956
[SPEAKER_02]: I forgot, it was a long time ago.

900
01:08:32,096 --> 01:08:37,480
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, the point is, the point is, they don't meet like that.

901
01:08:38,265 --> 01:08:38,725
[SPEAKER_00]: Really?

902
01:08:38,986 --> 01:08:41,267
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, even that nature of meeting is timeless.

903
01:08:41,507 --> 01:08:42,668
[SPEAKER_02]: It does not involve time.

904
01:08:43,288 --> 01:08:45,069
[SPEAKER_02]: Clapping hands involve time.

905
01:08:45,710 --> 01:08:46,210
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

906
01:08:46,710 --> 01:08:48,071
[SPEAKER_02]: You can put it as simply as that.

907
01:08:48,632 --> 01:08:52,174
[SPEAKER_02]: Saḷāyatana is part of the paticca upādāna.

908
01:08:52,334 --> 01:08:55,496
[SPEAKER_02]: Whatever is part of paticca upādāna shares the principle of paticca upādāna.

909
01:08:55,516 --> 01:08:56,997
[SPEAKER_02]: Otherwise, it wouldn't be included there.

910
01:08:58,658 --> 01:09:02,981
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it means that conscious matter is already there.

911
01:09:04,241 --> 01:09:05,322
[SPEAKER_00]: Conscious matter.

912
01:09:06,003 --> 01:09:06,283
[SPEAKER_02]: And if...

913
01:09:07,398 --> 01:09:12,543
[SPEAKER_02]: Things come in range, as the Buddha said, the corresponding engagement occurs.

914
01:09:13,324 --> 01:09:14,585
[SPEAKER_02]: But it doesn't occur always.

915
01:09:16,767 --> 01:09:20,370
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if things come in range, that can be the cause for the contact.

916
01:09:20,510 --> 01:09:24,594
[SPEAKER_02]: But then also, consciousness and the working sense organs, i.e.

917
01:09:24,634 --> 01:09:26,576
[SPEAKER_02]: matter, are required.

918
01:09:26,616 --> 01:09:33,522
[SPEAKER_02]: Because if something happens to them, there won't be any sight arising, there won't be any hearing or anything like that.

919
01:09:35,208 --> 01:09:40,594
[SPEAKER_02]: If your ear organs are damaged or whatever, even while you're still alive, that's it.

920
01:09:40,614 --> 01:09:41,414
[SPEAKER_02]: They'll stop working.

921
01:09:45,659 --> 01:09:58,292
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, like, there is... the Buddha did refer to a thing called āyur-saṅkhāra, that upon which life depends, like vitality.

922
01:09:59,401 --> 01:10:06,406
[SPEAKER_02]: So, sometimes you can be brain dead, that the body can be almost completely dysfunctional, but not quite.

923
01:10:08,808 --> 01:10:18,355
[SPEAKER_02]: And for as long as it's not quite, there might be still some vitality left in the body, in which case people wake up from the coma or something like that.

924
01:10:18,535 --> 01:10:20,096
[SPEAKER_02]: But sometimes they just don't.

925
01:10:22,098 --> 01:10:25,881
[SPEAKER_02]: Or there is a state of cessation of perception and feeling, where breathing stops.

926
01:10:27,093 --> 01:10:35,719
[SPEAKER_02]: In meditation, it's just the ultimate form of development of your mind, whereby, like, you don't even breathe.

927
01:10:36,400 --> 01:10:36,800
[SPEAKER_02]: That's it.

928
01:10:37,240 --> 01:10:55,193
[SPEAKER_02]: And the didas, the buddha, uh, no, sorry, it was the nun, dhamma dīna, when her ex, when her husband came to visit her, and she taught him, and he became sotāpanna, and he asked, what's the difference between somebody who is dead and somebody who is in cessation and perception of feeling?

929
01:10:55,773 --> 01:11:01,317
[SPEAKER_02]: And so, the difference is that the one who is in such an observational feeling, he still has his life vitality left.

930
01:11:01,978 --> 01:11:04,100
[SPEAKER_02]: So, he returns, he re-emerges.

931
01:11:04,400 --> 01:11:05,501
[SPEAKER_02]: The dead person doesn't.

932
01:11:06,962 --> 01:11:08,683
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's the difference.

933
01:11:08,723 --> 01:11:11,085
[SPEAKER_02]: It's almost like on the level of metaphysics.

934
01:11:11,145 --> 01:11:17,570
[SPEAKER_02]: But, yeah, the relationship between consciousness and body is external.

935
01:11:19,232 --> 01:11:20,693
[SPEAKER_02]: Consciousness is not internal in the body.

936
01:11:21,872 --> 01:11:28,933
[SPEAKER_02]: And I think the Buddha compared in the suttas like a ember, like a gem.

937
01:11:30,394 --> 01:11:36,975
[SPEAKER_02]: And if there is a thread running clearly through the gem, that's like conscious body.

938
01:11:37,015 --> 01:11:39,056
[SPEAKER_02]: But they are completely two different things.

939
01:11:40,676 --> 01:11:41,896
[SPEAKER_02]: Thread is not part of the gem.

940
01:11:41,936 --> 01:11:43,036
[SPEAKER_02]: Gem is not part of the thread.

941
01:11:43,156 --> 01:11:45,577
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just that it's entrapped in the gem.

942
01:11:46,368 --> 01:11:48,408
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's what Yanavira referred to, actually.

943
01:11:48,648 --> 01:11:51,129
[SPEAKER_02]: He didn't say that body and consciousness are superimposed.

944
01:11:51,149 --> 01:11:52,469
[SPEAKER_02]: He said they're juxtaposed.

945
01:11:53,369 --> 01:11:55,730
[SPEAKER_02]: It means they're completely indifferent to each other.

946
01:11:55,770 --> 01:12:02,371
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just... it's just an occurrence of them coming together, but not... not merging together.

947
01:12:03,371 --> 01:12:09,192
[SPEAKER_02]: They come together, and as a result of that overlap, you get nāmarūpa, that which you can designate.

948
01:12:09,912 --> 01:12:15,793
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you can never directly access matter, nor can you ever directly access consciousness.

949
01:12:17,162 --> 01:12:20,323
[SPEAKER_02]: You can abandon matter by fully understanding it.

950
01:12:21,083 --> 01:12:25,665
[SPEAKER_02]: Sorry, by fully understanding consciousness, you remove the footing for the matter.

951
01:12:27,666 --> 01:12:33,007
[SPEAKER_02]: In other words, you stop misunderstanding that overlap of conscious body.

952
01:12:34,848 --> 01:12:42,851
[SPEAKER_02]: But, well, it helped me and I think it's probably more helpful because everybody, by default, assumes consciousness is inside.

953
01:12:44,560 --> 01:12:45,840
[SPEAKER_02]: And everything else is external.

954
01:12:47,261 --> 01:12:48,301
[SPEAKER_02]: And I did actually remember that.

955
01:12:48,341 --> 01:13:08,126
[SPEAKER_02]: I didn't make much out of it at the time, but when I was reading Existential Philosophy, Sartre, and they were talking in terms of things were being... like, not things are conscious, in terms of that they have... that that matter has consciousness in it, but no, their consciousness is out there with the things, not in them.

956
01:13:11,152 --> 01:13:16,237
[SPEAKER_02]: In other words, that's why you have six types of consciousness, because you have six senses.

957
01:13:17,258 --> 01:13:23,364
[SPEAKER_02]: Every sense discerns things out there, and the only reason it can discern is because consciousness is there with those things.

958
01:13:28,370 --> 01:13:33,475
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the conscious matter, and then, if there is ignorance present,

959
01:13:34,568 --> 01:13:40,189
[SPEAKER_02]: When that contact arises, well, the contact will arise as a result of you feeling contacted.

960
01:13:40,870 --> 01:13:45,671
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you appropriate a contact, then you're contacted.

961
01:13:47,151 --> 01:13:49,292
[SPEAKER_02]: So, contact always pertains to sense of self.

962
01:13:50,752 --> 01:13:58,534
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you see that thing out there, already arisen, that the contact is better, means, oh, you can't do anything about it.

963
01:14:00,319 --> 01:14:05,142
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't prevent consciousness from disassociating from the matter, if it decides to do that.

964
01:14:07,123 --> 01:14:11,446
[SPEAKER_02]: Which is, I think, what happens when sometimes people lose their mind and they go catatonic.

965
01:14:11,746 --> 01:14:15,428
[SPEAKER_02]: They go completely, like, dead, motionless.

966
01:14:17,109 --> 01:14:20,231
[SPEAKER_02]: Nor can you prevent that matter to be destroyed.

967
01:14:20,251 --> 01:14:24,113
[SPEAKER_02]: You absolutely no... You can't even conceive having said in it.

968
01:14:25,154 --> 01:14:26,955
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just beyond your range.

969
01:14:27,615 --> 01:14:29,777
[SPEAKER_02]: Yet, your entire life depends upon it.

970
01:14:31,268 --> 01:14:32,768
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that's the perilous situation.

971
01:14:33,829 --> 01:14:41,190
[SPEAKER_02]: So, if you realize that your consciousness is as external as this matter that you see, and you have no say in it, then it ceases to be yours.

972
01:14:41,991 --> 01:14:53,153
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, when corresponding engagement occurs, you see that occurring as a result of things, that you have no say in it, therefore, whatever occurs on their account, you have no say in that either.

973
01:14:53,814 --> 01:14:56,094
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not yours.

974
01:14:56,114 --> 01:14:56,474
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

975
01:14:56,554 --> 01:14:59,895
[SPEAKER_00]: In this experience, in these episodes, how does it happen?

976
01:15:00,248 --> 01:15:05,750
[SPEAKER_00]: Some people think that there's a body in the... Well, I don't... yeah, hard to say, really.

977
01:15:05,910 --> 01:15:07,091
[SPEAKER_00]: It's quite a...

978
01:15:07,371 --> 01:15:10,792
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm sure something happens, but people use words differently.

979
01:15:11,152 --> 01:15:15,994
[SPEAKER_00]: They say they saw their body, but without any eyes, they can't... Yeah, yeah, I'd say that's the thing.

980
01:15:16,014 --> 01:15:16,634
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't believe.

981
01:15:17,054 --> 01:15:17,494
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't know.

982
01:15:17,594 --> 01:15:24,417
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not... it's like... As I say, it's hard to know what people mean when they describe these things.

983
01:15:25,369 --> 01:15:31,854
[SPEAKER_02]: Because, you know, they might use certain words that will mean something for you, something else for them.

984
01:15:32,935 --> 01:15:45,425
[SPEAKER_02]: So, when they say, I saw my body, you know, it might have well been a thought of their body, a thought of them abandoning the body, but because it was a real life and death experience, that thought was very significant.

985
01:15:45,746 --> 01:15:48,548
[SPEAKER_01]: It was so real, as they would usually describe it.

986
01:15:49,949 --> 01:15:53,492
[SPEAKER_02]: It was as real as if I was looking at my body over there.

987
01:15:54,775 --> 01:15:57,237
[SPEAKER_02]: It can be that, or who knows, really.

988
01:15:59,919 --> 01:16:08,165
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, when people even try to explain mundane things, often misunderstanding happens, because you realize they're using the same words you know, but completely differently.

989
01:16:08,205 --> 01:16:09,686
[SPEAKER_02]: They meant something completely different.

990
01:16:10,486 --> 01:16:21,174
[SPEAKER_02]: Imagine when you try to explain such personal things, or like, when people try to talk about, you know, the spiritual thoughts or whatever else, and he gets all like, what?

991
01:16:21,867 --> 01:16:26,989
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't make sense out of it, because what it means for them doesn't necessarily mean other things to others.

992
01:16:28,430 --> 01:16:34,453
[SPEAKER_02]: But that... that's exactly why the Buddha did refer to the miracle of teaching.

993
01:16:35,433 --> 01:16:44,037
[SPEAKER_02]: And he said, if a person who understood the dhamma is able to convey that to another, and then they understand it, that's... that equals a miracle.

994
01:16:46,279 --> 01:16:46,479
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

995
01:16:46,539 --> 01:16:49,040
[SPEAKER_02]: Because the dhamma is so personal.

996
01:16:50,436 --> 01:17:02,105
[SPEAKER_02]: The sense itself, the senses and everything, that there is no way that when I say something in regard to senses, another person will think exactly the same thing, unless we're both arahants or something like that.

997
01:17:03,286 --> 01:17:06,428
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it's so personal that the meaning of it is so personal.

998
01:17:07,128 --> 01:17:11,232
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it really needs to be... you need to dig through it and see what it's meant.

999
01:17:11,612 --> 01:17:17,757
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, I think Amāli said before, again, it's like when I said, well, you can just take these five aggregates, Pali terms,

1000
01:17:18,933 --> 01:17:21,974
[SPEAKER_02]: rūpa, vedanā, sañña, saṅkhāra, viññāṇa.

1001
01:17:21,994 --> 01:17:29,057
[SPEAKER_02]: Just take the English words, or Japanese words for that, and think about it, and see how much of it do you actually understand.

1002
01:17:30,937 --> 01:17:45,903
[SPEAKER_02]: I remember when I was reading the suttas and reading consciousness, and you just really know you have consciousness, but then you stop once and think, well, okay, forget about the Pali, forget about the sutta, just use the word consciousness, that I think I know,

1003
01:17:47,479 --> 01:17:47,839
[SPEAKER_02]: What is it?

1004
01:17:48,520 --> 01:17:55,943
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, when I say consciousness, what is that, that this designation consciousness stands for, in my experience?

1005
01:17:56,343 --> 01:17:56,744
[SPEAKER_02]: What is it?

1006
01:17:57,624 --> 01:17:59,685
[SPEAKER_02]: And then you realize, okay, consciousness is very hard.

1007
01:18:00,225 --> 01:18:01,926
[SPEAKER_02]: Why don't I start with easy matter?

1008
01:18:02,946 --> 01:18:05,808
[SPEAKER_02]: And then you realize that's equally hard.

1009
01:18:07,248 --> 01:18:08,409
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what is that matter then?

1010
01:18:08,869 --> 01:18:14,252
[SPEAKER_02]: And you realize, if you were to fully understand these five designations, you would be an arahant.

1011
01:18:15,773 --> 01:18:20,295
[SPEAKER_02]: Because there is no difference between intellectual understanding or real understanding.

1012
01:18:20,335 --> 01:18:21,455
[SPEAKER_02]: Understanding is understanding.

1013
01:18:21,935 --> 01:18:27,757
[SPEAKER_02]: You can start intellectually or non-intellectually, but understanding that it's the same arrival.

1014
01:18:28,938 --> 01:18:30,498
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you might start pondering about it.

1015
01:18:31,018 --> 01:18:40,742
[SPEAKER_02]: And as long as you're not doing like scholastic comparison between the words and its roots, if you're really trying to see what designation consciousness stands in your experience,

1016
01:18:42,351 --> 01:18:50,095
[SPEAKER_02]: You can just... That's why in the suttas, if you read something, when they say a thing twice, when they say, first jhāna, first jhāna, what is first jhāna?

1017
01:18:51,135 --> 01:18:53,016
[SPEAKER_02]: Or they say something else, they repeat it twice.

1018
01:18:53,476 --> 01:18:55,517
[SPEAKER_02]: That's basically a way of bringing it to the mind.

1019
01:18:56,437 --> 01:19:01,980
[SPEAKER_02]: And then to see that, that you just brought to your mind, in your experience, for what it stands for.

1020
01:19:04,742 --> 01:19:09,565
[SPEAKER_02]: Not just kind of examine this code and explain it, or draw the parallel, do the drawing, or whatever, a scheme.

1021
01:19:10,225 --> 01:19:17,429
[SPEAKER_02]: But, like, really understand if consciousness-consciousness that I just said, what is that what I said?

1022
01:19:18,070 --> 01:19:20,611
[SPEAKER_02]: What is it that this saying stands for?

1023
01:19:20,951 --> 01:19:21,432
[SPEAKER_02]: What is that?

1024
01:19:22,452 --> 01:19:27,215
[SPEAKER_02]: What did I just designate, in my experience, when I said feeling?

1025
01:19:27,455 --> 01:19:28,716
[SPEAKER_02]: What is feeling now that I said it?

1026
01:19:29,942 --> 01:19:32,603
[SPEAKER_02]: And the fact that you said feeling, means it stands for something.

1027
01:19:32,883 --> 01:19:33,964
[SPEAKER_02]: It designates something.

1028
01:19:34,644 --> 01:19:43,107
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you really have to push as hard as... well, as far as you need to, until same feeling stands for that which feeling really is.

1029
01:19:44,548 --> 01:19:49,930
[SPEAKER_02]: Not for your idea of what feeling is, or for your misunderstanding of what feeling is.

1030
01:19:50,430 --> 01:19:54,072
[SPEAKER_02]: And you can see how many people have not understood this,

1031
01:19:55,395 --> 01:20:01,800
[SPEAKER_02]: By seeing how many people cannot distinguish between what they perceive and what they feel, or what they intend.

1032
01:20:02,681 --> 01:20:04,302
[SPEAKER_02]: And it's all like a like a like a jungle.

1033
01:20:04,622 --> 01:20:07,164
[SPEAKER_02]: Just everything and everything.

1034
01:20:08,565 --> 01:20:09,245
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it that...

1035
01:20:09,305 --> 01:20:17,492
[SPEAKER_00]: I remember that you sent me that like a notes on meditation, something, your disciple wrote something there, notes on notes on meditation.

1036
01:20:17,512 --> 01:20:19,233
[SPEAKER_02]: No, that was dukkha, on dukkha.

1037
01:20:21,062 --> 01:20:32,604
[SPEAKER_00]: It was written that the difference, the perception, the difference between the arahant and the kutu-jana is that arahant, when he sees the chair, he doesn't think it is his.

1038
01:20:32,624 --> 01:20:35,285
[SPEAKER_00]: But kutu-jana thinks it is his chair.

1039
01:20:35,385 --> 01:20:36,505
[SPEAKER_00]: Only that is the difference.

1040
01:20:36,605 --> 01:20:36,865
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it?

1041
01:20:37,365 --> 01:20:39,225
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, it's slightly subtler than that.

1042
01:20:39,345 --> 01:20:43,266
[SPEAKER_02]: He might not think that's my chair, because if it's his chair, I won't think it's my chair.

1043
01:20:44,146 --> 01:20:47,467
[SPEAKER_02]: But I can think that chair is for me to sit on.

1044
01:20:48,467 --> 01:20:48,967
[SPEAKER_00]: For me?

1045
01:20:49,167 --> 01:20:49,427
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

1046
01:20:53,431 --> 01:20:55,293
[SPEAKER_02]: It's for me to sit on.

1047
01:20:56,073 --> 01:21:01,057
[SPEAKER_02]: But the chair, the significance of the chair, is for sitting.

1048
01:21:01,337 --> 01:21:02,478
[SPEAKER_02]: It's to be sat on.

1049
01:21:04,279 --> 01:21:06,180
[SPEAKER_02]: And that doesn't necessarily mean sense of self.

1050
01:21:07,041 --> 01:21:13,165
[SPEAKER_02]: But when somebody who holds sense of self and self-view approaches it, he will inevitably take it like that.

1051
01:21:14,246 --> 01:21:19,270
[SPEAKER_02]: So, when a patujana sees a chair, or any other signi... a cup,

1052
01:21:20,217 --> 01:21:21,398
[SPEAKER_02]: To drink water from?

1053
01:21:22,279 --> 01:21:26,663
[SPEAKER_02]: It will be... a cup is for my eye to drink water from.

1054
01:21:26,683 --> 01:21:29,085
[SPEAKER_02]: It's for myself, for me, to drink water from.

1055
01:21:29,646 --> 01:21:31,388
[SPEAKER_02]: The chair is for me to sit on.

1056
01:21:31,708 --> 01:21:32,549
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's already a sense of self.

1057
01:21:32,569 --> 01:21:32,729
[SPEAKER_02]: For me.

1058
01:21:32,749 --> 01:21:40,316
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you put... you put... you put... you put... you put... you put... you put... you put... you put... you put... you put... you put... you put... you put... you put... you put... you put... you put... you put... you put... you put... you put... you put... you put... you put... you put... you put... you put... you put

1059
01:21:41,231 --> 01:21:42,132
[SPEAKER_00]: What's happening?

1060
01:21:42,432 --> 01:21:43,893
[SPEAKER_00]: I never think that it's my river.

1061
01:21:44,333 --> 01:21:55,399
[SPEAKER_02]: That's fine, but as I said, seeing the river outside, it's only a particular object within the general situation that you're in, and yourself is on the general level.

1062
01:21:55,699 --> 01:21:59,381
[SPEAKER_02]: You'll never see your sense of self on the level where you're seeing.

1063
01:22:00,361 --> 01:22:06,825
[SPEAKER_02]: Your sense of self is in that peripheral situation that determines everything particular that's right in front of you.

1064
01:22:07,939 --> 01:22:13,420
[SPEAKER_02]: That's why people never... can never understand anatta when they go about and say, ah, that's not real, that doesn't exist.

1065
01:22:13,440 --> 01:22:14,680
[SPEAKER_02]: See, it's so constantly changing.

1066
01:22:14,720 --> 01:22:15,481
[SPEAKER_02]: I just destroyed it.

1067
01:22:15,501 --> 01:22:16,501
[SPEAKER_02]: It means it's impermanent.

1068
01:22:17,121 --> 01:22:19,781
[SPEAKER_02]: Those are all objects of your senses.

1069
01:22:19,801 --> 01:22:21,982
[SPEAKER_02]: And your sense of self is way deeper than that.

1070
01:22:22,782 --> 01:22:25,162
[SPEAKER_02]: It's on the level of that situation I was telling you about.

1071
01:22:26,143 --> 01:22:26,243
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

1072
01:22:26,303 --> 01:22:26,443
[SPEAKER_00]: I...

1073
01:22:27,123 --> 01:22:27,523
[SPEAKER_00]: I...

